<#
.SYNOPSIS
    Suppresses heartbeat alerting for SCOM agents that have been gracefully
    shut down (Event ID 1074), for as long as they stay down.

.DESCRIPTION
    Runs on a management server. Nothing is installed on the agents.
    Built for a large management group (~3500 agents) where 60-400 machines
    may be gracefully down at once, for days or weeks at a time.

    DESIGN
      Maintenance mode is applied in a BOUNDED window and rolled forward while
      the machine stays down, rather than set once for a long period. If this
      script stops running, machines surface as normal heartbeat alerts within
      MaintenanceHours instead of staying invisible indefinitely.

      Machines currently suppressed are tracked in a local state file, so no
      pass ever enumerates the Windows Computer class. Cost is proportional to
      the number of machines actually down, not to the size of the group.

    THREE SCHEDULED TASKS
      APPLY - every 5 minutes. Loops internally so a pass runs every 60s.
        powershell.exe -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass ^
          -File "D:\Scripts\Set-ScomShutdownMaintenanceMode.ps1" ^
          -LoopIntervalSeconds 60 -LoopBudgetSeconds 240 -LockTimeoutSeconds 20

      SWEEP - every 15 minutes. Releases machines that are back, extends MM
              for those still down, reapplies lapsed MM, records events.
        powershell.exe -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass ^
          -File "D:\Scripts\Set-ScomShutdownMaintenanceMode.ps1" -Sweep

      DIGEST - weekly. Emails the dormant-machine review and the suppression
               summary, and prunes the event history.
        powershell.exe -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass ^
          -File "D:\Scripts\Set-ScomShutdownMaintenanceMode.ps1" -Digest

    All three take a machine-wide mutex per pass, so they cannot overlap and
    clobber each other's view of the state file. The mutex is held for the
    duration of the WORK, not the process - under the APPLY loop, holding it
    for the whole invocation would starve SWEEP out completely.

    The internal loop is APPLY-only. SWEEP, DIGEST, CHANGE and -DryRun always
    run a single pass and ignore -LoopIntervalSeconds, so it is safe to set
    LoopIntervalSeconds as the parameter default instead of on the APPLY
    command line. When the loop is on and -LockTimeoutSeconds is not given, the
    lock timeout drops to a third of the interval (20s at 60s).

    EXCLUSIONS
      Machines named in -ExclusionFile are never put into maintenance mode by
      any mode of this script - management servers above all. One entry per
      line, '#' starts a comment, wildcards * and ? allowed. Each entry is
      matched, case-insensitively, against the FQDN and the short host name:

          SCOMMS01                    one server, by short name
          scomgw01.contoso.com        one server, by FQDN
          ???????MGT????              every server with role code MGT

        APPLY  - an excluded machine's shutdown alert is left OPEN, so the
                 shutdown is visible in the console. Not tracked.
        SWEEP  - a machine added to the list after it was suppressed is taken
                 out of this script's window (up or down) and dropped from
                 tracking. Someone else's window is left alone.
        CHANGE - excluded CIs are left out of change windows. A window that has
                 already started is never touched, so remove one by hand.

      The file is re-read whenever it changes, so edits apply on the next pass.
      No file = nothing excluded (logged). A file that exists but cannot be
      read reuses the last good copy; with no good copy the pass is skipped
      rather than risk putting an excluded machine into maintenance mode.

    LATENCY
      Heartbeat at 6 x 60s gives roughly a 6-minute budget from last heartbeat
      to a heartbeat failure alert. A 5-minute task with 60-second internal
      iterations puts worst-case alert-to-maintenance-mode at about 90 seconds.
      Task Scheduler repetition intervals are not used at all.

    AGENT LIVENESS
      Release is confirmed by a TCP connect to the agent channel port (5723).
      That single round trip proves the machine is up AND that HealthService is
      listening - no RPC, no local admin on 3500 agents, and immune to the
      DHCP-address-reuse false positive that plain ping has.

      A probe wave costs ONE PingTimeoutMs plus ONE TcpTimeoutMs, not one per
      machine: every probe is started before anything is waited on, and the
      harvest loop runs against a single deadline per protocol. This matters
      more here than it looks. ConnectAsync has no timeout of its own, so a
      switched-off host on a network that does not answer with ICMP unreachable
      sits in SYN retransmit for ~21s - and switched-off hosts are this
      script's entire tracked population.

    OTHER PEOPLE'S MAINTENANCE WINDOWS
      Before extending or releasing anything, the sweep asks who set the window
      it is looking at, by reading $Marker back out of the window comment. A
      window this script did not set is left strictly alone: not extended, not
      released, and if the machine comes back it is dropped from tracking with
      the window still in place rather than being pulled out of it. A machine
      still down when a foreign window lapses is picked up again by the normal
      reapply path, which is why hands-off machines stay tracked.

      If window comments cannot be read on your SDK build, ownership comes back
      Unknown and every window is treated as this script's own - behaviour is
      then exactly what it was before ownership existed. That direction is
      deliberate: treating unreadable as foreign would stop the script
      extending or releasing anything at all, and the tracked population would
      climb to TrackedCeiling with the estate blind behind it.

      A BLANK comment is foreign once any window has proved comments are
      readable (every window this script sets carries the marker); before that
      proof it is Unknown. Either way it no longer switches ownership off for
      the whole run, which it used to do if it was the first window read.

    STATE LOSS AND RECOVERY
      Set-TrackedState writes through a temporary file and swaps it in with
      File.Replace, which leaves the previous generation at <state>.bak. If the
      primary is ever missing or unparseable, the next pass rebuilds from that
      backup, restores the primary, and emails the recovery notice - at most one
      pass of changes is lost, and the next sweep re-derives those from SCOM. A
      corrupt primary is kept at <state>.corrupt rather than being overwritten.
      Only when the backup is missing, empty or unreadable as well does this
      become the StateLoss emergency: machines suppressed, untracked, and
      invisible until their windows expire.

    DRY RUN
      -DryRun queries alerts, resolves computers, probes agents and works out
      ownership, prints the plan, and stops. No maintenance mode, no alert
      resolution states, no state files, no mail, and deliberately no watchdog event
      - the watchdog rule fires on the ABSENCE of those, so a dry run emitting
      one would falsely report that a script which changed nothing was working.
      Single-pass: -LoopIntervalSeconds is ignored so a task command line can be
      pasted verbatim with -DryRun appended. Exits 0.

        powershell.exe -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass ^
          -File "D:\Scripts\Shutdown.ps1" -Sweep -DryRun

    NOTIFICATIONS
      Mail is OFF unless -SmtpServer, -MailFrom and -MailTo are all supplied.
      The script emails only where email is genuinely the right channel:
        - a systemic fault that looks like success from the outside
          (runaway suppression, maintenance mode failing wholesale, state loss)
        - the weekly digests, which go to people who do not live in the console
      Everything else is a Windows event for a SCOM rule to pick up. Notice
      that "the script stopped running" is deliberately NOT an email - a dead
      script cannot send one. That is what the watchdog events below are for.

    WATCHDOG EVENTS  (Application log, source $EventSource, one per invocation)
                  done, clean    done, with errors   did no work
        APPLY       9100 Info       9101 Warning       9102 Error
        SWEEP       9110 Info       9111 Warning       9112 Error
        DIGEST      9120 Info       9121 Warning       9122 Error
        CHANGE      9130 Info       9131 Warning       9132 Error

      "Did no work" = the SCOM module did not load, the management group could
      not be reached, or every pass threw. It used to be reported as success
      when the lock was never won.

      An invocation where every iteration DEFERRED to the other task holding the
      mutex is reported as x101 (warning), NOT x102. A sweep that holds the lock
      for longer than the whole APPLY budget is a busy management server, not a
      broken script, and x102 there fires the did-no-work rule on a system where
      nothing is wrong. The message names the cause and gives the skip count.
      x103 is unused if you would rather alert on it separately - add it to the
      Missing Event monitor as well if you split it out.

      Each mode has its own IDs so each task can be watched on its own: a SCOM
      Missing Event monitor per task, on x100 OR x101, over about three times
      that task's schedule. With a shared ID the 5-minute APPLY task kept the
      watchdog satisfied while SWEEP was not running at all. A task that dies
      before it can log anything (a bad parameter, say) writes nothing, which
      is exactly what the Missing Event monitor catches. A simple alert rule
      on x102 says WHY, straight away.

    SDK SURFACE - the part that is easy to get wrong
      Start   Start-SCOMMaintenanceMode -Instance <MonitoringObject[]> -EndTime <local>
              (batched cmdlet; falls back to ScheduleMaintenanceMode per object)
      Extend  Get-SCOMMaintenanceMode -Instance <MonitoringObject[]>
                | Set-SCOMMaintenanceMode -MaintenanceModeEntry <MaintenanceWindow[]>
              (Set-SCOMMaintenanceMode does NOT take -Instance)
      Stop    $instance.StopMaintenanceMode(<utc>, TraversalDepth::Recursive)
              There is no Stop-SCOMMaintenanceMode cmdlet. It has never existed.
      Cmdlets take LOCAL time. SDK methods take UTC. Everything here is computed
      in UTC and converted only at the cmdlet boundary.

    VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING AT SCALE
      1. Start-SCOMMaintenanceMode has no TraversalDepth parameter. Put one
         machine in MM with it, then confirm in the console that the HEALTH
         SERVICE object under that Windows Computer is also in maintenance
         mode - that specifically is what the Health Service Watcher checks
         before raising heartbeat failure. If it is not, set UseSdkForApply.
      2. The Windows Computer lookup filters on FullName starting
         'Microsoft.Windows.Computer:'. Confirm one lookup resolves; there is
         an empty-Path fallback if your instances are shaped differently.
      3. Alert.PrincipalName should carry the agent FQDN for your 1074 rule.
         Log a pass with -Verbose and confirm the FQDNs look right.
      4. Probe one known-good agent, one down agent, and one machine with
         HealthService deliberately stopped, and confirm the three outcomes.
      5. Ownership and window end times both come from GetMaintenanceWindow().
         Put one machine in maintenance mode BY HAND with a comment of your own,
         run -Sweep -DryRun, and confirm it reports HANDS-OFF. If the log says
         ownership cannot be determined, this SDK build does not surface window
         comments and the feature is inert - safely, but inert.

    Requires the OperationsManager module (Windows PowerShell 5.1 - the module
    does not load in PowerShell 7).

.NOTES
    Event source registration needs one elevated run:
        New-EventLog -LogName Application -Source 'ScomShutdownMM'
    The script attempts this itself and degrades quietly if it cannot.
#>
#Requires -Version 5.1
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
    # Array, so a dead management server fails over to the next rather than
    # losing the whole invocation.
    [string[]]$ManagementServer = @('localhost'),

    # Display name of the alert raised by your 1074 rule.
    [string]$AlertName = 'System Shutdown Event 1074 Alert',

    # Bounded MM window, rolled forward by the sweep while the machine is down.
    # This is your blast radius if the sweep stops running.
    [ValidateRange(1, 720)]
    [int]$MaintenanceHours = 24,

    # Extend MM when its remaining life drops below this. The gap between this
    # and MaintenanceHours is how many failed sweeps you can absorb.
    [ValidateRange(1, 719)]
    [int]$ExtendWhenWithinHours = 8,

    # Apply mode: only consider alerts raised inside this window. Processed
    # alerts are stamped closed, so the resolution state is the real dedupe -
    # this window only stops the first run sweeping up ancient history. It can
    # be generous, and needs to be wider than a burst takes to drain at MaxBatch.
    [ValidateRange(5, 10080)]
    [int]$LookbackMinutes = 240,

    # Ceiling on machines entering MM per pass. MM start/stop triggers config
    # recalculation, so a 390-machine burst is better spread over a few passes.
    [ValidateRange(1, 1000)]
    [int]$MaxBatch = 100,

    # Ceiling on machines LEAVING MM per sweep. Monday morning is the mass
    # event - hundreds returning at once, each an individual SDK stop call.
    [ValidateRange(1, 1000)]
    [int]$MaxReleaseBatch = 100,

    # Resolution state stamped on processed alerts. 255 = Closed.
    [ValidateRange(0, 255)]
    [int]$ProcessedResolutionState = 255,

    # Sweep mode: release / extend / reapply / record.
    [switch]$Sweep,

    # Digest mode: weekly report email, and event-history pruning.
    [switch]$Digest,

    # Machines suppressed longer than this are reported for human review.
    # Nothing is removed automatically.
    [ValidateRange(1, 3650)]
    [int]$ReviewAfterDays = 14,

    [ValidateRange(1, 3650)]
    [int]$ReportEveryDays = 7,

    [ValidateRange(100, 30000)]
    [int]$PingTimeoutMs = 2000,

    [ValidateRange(100, 30000)]
    [int]$DnsTimeoutMs = 3000,

    # ---- Agent liveness probe --------------------------------------------
    # A successful TCP connect proves the machine is up AND HealthService is
    # listening, in one round trip.
    [ValidateRange(1, 65535)]
    [int]$AgentPort = 5723,

    [ValidateRange(250, 30000)]
    [int]$TcpTimeoutMs = 3000,

    # Machines probed per DNS/ping/TCP wave. Everything inside a wave is in
    # flight at once, so a wave costs one slow probe in TIME but holds a socket
    # and a ping handle per machine while it runs. Caps the fan-out now that the
    # tracked population can legitimately reach TrackedCeiling.
    [ValidateRange(10, 2000)]
    [int]$ProbeBatchSize = 250,

    # Falls back to the ping-streak rule. The old -SkipHealthServiceCheck name
    # is kept as an alias so existing task command lines do not break.
    [Alias('SkipHealthServiceCheck')]
    [switch]$SkipAgentPortCheck,

    # Consecutive pings needed to release when the port probe is disabled.
    [ValidateRange(1, 20)]
    [int]$PingStreakForRelease = 2,

    # A machine answering ping but not $AgentPort is normally mid-boot. After
    # this many consecutive sweeps it is released anyway, so a genuinely broken
    # agent surfaces as a heartbeat alert instead of sitting in MM forever.
    # At a 15-minute sweep, 4 = one hour of boot tolerance.
    # Set very high to restore "stay suppressed until the agent answers".
    [ValidateRange(1, 9999)]
    [int]$ReleaseAfterPingOnlySweeps = 4,

    # Do everything except change anything: query alerts, resolve computers,
    # probe agents, work out ownership, then print the plan and stop. No
    # maintenance mode, no alert updates, no state files, no mail, and no
    # watchdog event - a dry run must not satisfy the SCOM rule watching for
    # the ABSENCE of the x100/x101 events, because it has not done the job.
    [switch]$DryRun,

    # Use the SDK ScheduleMaintenanceMode (explicit Recursive) instead of the
    # Start-SCOMMaintenanceMode cmdlet. Slower - one call per machine.
    [switch]$UseSdkForApply,

    # ---- Verification settling -------------------------------------------
    # Every MM write is verified by re-reading the instance, because "the call
    # did not throw" is not proof it took. A read on the SAME TICK as the write
    # can still see the pre-write state, which then gets logged as a hard MM
    # failure - and at MmFailureAlertCount of those in one pass, mailed as
    # "maintenance mode failing wholesale". A Monday-morning batch of a hundred
    # releases is exactly where that happens. Wait this long before the read
    # that decides. 0 restores the old same-tick behaviour.
    [ValidateRange(0, 30000)]
    [int]$VerifySettleMs = 2000,

    # Anything that still does not read as expected gets ONE more look after
    # this. Only the stragglers are re-read, so a pass where everything took
    # cleanly - the normal case - costs nothing at all.
    [ValidateRange(0, 60000)]
    [int]$VerifyRetryMs = 5000,

    # ---- Internal loop ----------------------------------------------------
    # Run a pass every LoopIntervalSeconds until LoopBudgetSeconds has elapsed,
    # then exit. Lets a 5-minute task deliver 1-minute latency without relying
    # on Task Scheduler repetition, and pays the module-import and connection
    # cost once per invocation instead of once per pass.
    # 0 = single pass. APPLY only: SWEEP, DIGEST, CHANGE and -DryRun ignore it
    # and run once, so it can safely be hard-coded here as the default.
    [ValidateRange(0, 300)]
    [int]$LoopIntervalSeconds = 0,

    # Stop starting new iterations past this. Must leave room for the last pass
    # to finish before the task fires again: 240 on a 5-minute task leaves ~60s.
    [ValidateRange(30, 3600)]
    [int]$LoopBudgetSeconds = 240,

    # ---- CHANGE mode ------------------------------------------------------
    # Reads approved, CI-impacting changes from the Service Manager database and
    # creates a SCOM maintenance SCHEDULE per change, so SCOM itself starts the
    # window at DOWN_START and ends it at PLANNED_END. Nothing is generated and
    # nothing has to be run at outage time.
    #
    # REQUIRES the Operations Manager Administrator role. Maintenance schedules
    # do; plain maintenance mode does not. The pass fails cleanly and loudly if
    # the account does not have it.
    [switch]$Change,

    [string]$ChangeSqlServer = '',
    [string]$ChangeDatabase  = '',

    # Only CIs whose CI_NAME matches this are considered.
    [string]$ChangeCiFilter  = 'diep',

    # CMDB CI names are often short. Tried as given first, then with this
    # appended, because SCOM display names are usually FQDNs.
    [string]$ChangeDomainSuffix = $env:USERDNSDOMAIN,

    [ValidateRange(1, 365)]
    [int]$ChangeLookAheadDays = 14,

    [ValidateRange(1, 5000)]
    [int]$ChangeMaxRows = 200,

    [ValidateRange(1, 1440)]
    [int]$ChangeMinDurationMinutes = 5,

    # Sanity cap against bad data in PLANNED_END.
    [ValidateRange(1, 8760)]
    [int]$ChangeMaxDurationHours = 72,

    # Schedules this script owns are named <prefix><change number>. Anything
    # not matching the prefix is somebody else's and is never touched.
    [string]$ChangeSchedulePrefix = 'CHM-',

    # Time zone ActiveStartTime is interpreted in. VERIFY on the first run that
    # the window lands where you expect.
    [string]$ChangeTimeZoneId = ([System.TimeZoneInfo]::Local.Id),

    # Remove schedules for changes that are no longer approved and open. Only
    # ever removes windows that have NOT started, that this script holds state
    # for, and that have stayed missing for ChangeWithdrawAfterMinutes.
    [switch]$ChangeReconcile,

    # A change can drop out of the query for a moment - data lag, an approval
    # state transition, a filter change. One missing observation is not evidence
    # the change is gone, so a window is only withdrawn after it has stayed
    # missing this long. 0 withdraws on the first missing pass.
    [ValidateRange(0, 1440)]
    [int]$ChangeWithdrawAfterMinutes = 30,

    # Slack either side of a change window during which the shutdown side still
    # treats the machine as hands-off, so the two never race at the boundary.
    [ValidateRange(0, 720)]
    [int]$ChangeWindowGraceMinutes = 15,

    # ---- Paths ------------------------------------------------------------
    [string]$StateFile       = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomShutdownMM.csv',
    [string]$ChangeStateFile = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomChangeWindows.csv',
    [string]$NotifyStateFile = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomShutdownMM.notify.json',

    # CHANGE mode's own notification state. It runs under a DIFFERENT mutex from
    # APPLY/SWEEP/DIGEST, so one shared read-modify-write file is the last place
    # the two sides can still clobber each other - and LastTrackedCount lives in
    # it, which is what Test-StateLoss compares against to tell real state loss
    # from a legitimate drain to zero. Separate files, no race.
    [string]$ChangeNotifyStateFile = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomChangeMM.notify.json',
    [string]$EventCsvPath    = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomShutdownMM.events.csv',
    [string]$ReportPath      = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomDormantAgents.csv',
    [string]$LogPath         = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomShutdownMM.log',

    # Machines this script must NEVER put into maintenance mode - management
    # servers above all. See EXCLUSIONS above for the format. '' turns the
    # feature off; a missing file means nothing is excluded.
    [string]$ExclusionFile   = 'C:\Temp\Martin\AutoMaintV2\ScomShutdownMM.Exclusions.txt',

    [ValidateRange(1MB, 512MB)]
    [long]$LogMaxBytes = 20MB,

    [ValidateRange(1, 50)]
    [int]$LogKeep = 5,

    [ValidateRange(7, 3650)]
    [int]$EventRetentionDays = 90,

    # Seconds to wait for the other task to finish before giving up this pass.
    # Keep this well under LoopIntervalSeconds so a slow sweep costs one
    # iteration rather than the whole window. If not given while the APPLY loop
    # is on, it defaults to a third of LoopIntervalSeconds (max 20).
    [ValidateRange(1, 600)]
    [int]$LockTimeoutSeconds = 90,

    # ---- Windows event log (the watchdog channel) -------------------------
    [string]$EventLogName = 'Application',
    [string]$EventSource  = 'ScomShutdownMM',
    [switch]$NoEventLog,

    # ---- Mail -------------------------------------------------------------
    # Everything below is inert unless SmtpServer, MailFrom and MailTo are set.
    [string]$SmtpServer = 'SMTPRelay.diep.mil.au',
    [ValidateRange(1, 65535)]
    [int]$SmtpPort = 25,
    [switch]$SmtpUseSsl,

    # Path to a PSCredential exported with Export-Clixml. DPAPI-encrypted and
    # bound to the exporting ACCOUNT and MACHINE - re-export if either changes.
    # Leave empty for an anonymous / IP-allowlisted relay.
    [string]$SmtpCredentialPath = '',

    [ValidateRange(1000, 120000)]
    [int]$SmtpTimeoutMs = 15000,

    [string]$MailFrom = 'DIEP-SCOM@DoNotReply.defence.gov.au',

    # Operational faults - you / the SCOM team.
    [string[]]$MailTo = @('martin.birrell1@defence.gov.au'),

    # Digests - asset owners, service owners. Falls back to MailTo if empty.
    [string[]]$MailReportTo = @('martin.birrell1@defence.gov.au'),

    [string]$MailSubjectPrefix = '[SCOM-MM]',

    # After a failed send, retry that notification this soon rather than
    # waiting out its full interval.
    [ValidateRange(1, 240)]
    [int]$FailureRetryMinutes = 5,

    # ---- Notification thresholds -----------------------------------------
    # Total machines suppressed at once. A HARD fence, not just an alarm: at or
    # above this, no further machine enters maintenance mode this pass and its
    # alert is left open to surface normally. Set well above your real peak - a
    # runaway blinds the monitoring for a whole MaintenanceHours window while
    # looking perfectly healthy from the log, and a genuine outage larger than
    # this ceiling will alert rather than be suppressed. That is the trade.
    [ValidateRange(1, 100000)]
    [int]$TrackedCeiling = 600,

    # Machines entering MM in a SINGLE pass. 0 = off. Turn this on once you
    # know what your normal burst looks like; legitimate weekend bursts can
    # run at MaxBatch for several passes.
    [ValidateRange(0, 1000)]
    [int]$ApplyBurstCeiling = 0,

    # Maintenance mode failing for this many machines in one pass means
    # something systemic (rights, SDK, config service), not bad luck.
    [ValidateRange(1, 1000)]
    [int]$MmFailureAlertCount = 5,

    # State file GONE while this many machines were tracked = state loss.
    # A file that merely reads empty is a legitimate drain and is not alerted.
    [ValidateRange(1, 10000)]
    [int]$StateLossThreshold = 10,

    # Fraction of tracked machines answering ping with nothing on AgentPort
    # before this is treated as a blocked port rather than broken agents.
    [ValidateRange(0.1, 1.0)]
    [double]$PortBlindRatio = 0.8
)

$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
$Marker = 'AutoMM-Shutdown-1074'

$script:Errs             = 0     # non-zero exit if anything failed
$script:StateFileExisted = $true
$script:StateRecoveredFrom = $null   # set by Get-TrackedState when .bak was used
$script:LogRotateChecked = $false
$script:LockSkips        = 0     # iterations that deferred to the other task
$script:EventSourceOk    = $null
$script:SmtpCred         = $null
$script:MailWarned       = $false

# $null = not yet probed, $true = window comments are readable and ownership
# can be decided, $false = they are not, so every window is treated as ours and
# behaviour is exactly as it was before ownership existed. See Get-MaintenanceOwner.
$script:MmCommentReadable = $null

# $null = not yet probed, $true = the SDK gives us maintenance window end times,
# $false = it does not and extends fall back to confirming InMaintenanceMode
# alone. See Get-MaintenanceEndUtc.
$script:MmWindowReadable = $null

# How this SDK build reports a window end whose DateTimeKind is Unspecified.
# $null = not yet established, 'Utc' or 'Local' once a window we set ourselves
# has settled it. See Register-MaintenanceWindowKind.
$script:MmWindowKind       = $null
$script:MmWindowKindWarned = $false

# Accumulated across every iteration of one invocation, reported in the
# closing Windows event.
$script:Stats = [ordered]@{
    Passes     = 0
    Applied    = 0
    Released   = 0
    Extended   = 0
    Reapplied  = 0
    MmFailures = 0
    Orphans    = 0
    PingOnly   = 0
    Tracked    = 0
    ChgCreated = 0
    ChgAmended = 0
    ChgRemoved = 0
    Excluded   = 0
}

# Exclusion list. $null = not loaded yet this invocation. See Get-ExclusionList.
$script:Exclusions       = $null
$script:ExclusionStamp   = $null
$script:ExclusionWarned  = $false
$script:ExcludedLogged   = @{}     # FQDNs already logged this invocation

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Startup validation
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
if ($PSVersionTable.PSEdition -eq 'Core') {
    throw 'This script requires Windows PowerShell 5.1. The OperationsManager module does not load in PowerShell 7.'
}

if ($ExtendWhenWithinHours -ge $MaintenanceHours) {
    throw ("ExtendWhenWithinHours ({0}) must be less than MaintenanceHours ({1}); otherwise every sweep extends every machine and you churn agent config for nothing." -f
           $ExtendWhenWithinHours, $MaintenanceHours)
}

if ($Sweep -and $Digest) {
    throw 'Use -Sweep or -Digest, not both.'
}

if ($Change -and ($Sweep -or $Digest)) {
    throw 'Use -Change on its own. It is a separate task from -Sweep and -Digest.'
}

if ($Change) {
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ChangeSqlServer) -or [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ChangeDatabase)) {
        throw '-Change needs -ChangeSqlServer and -ChangeDatabase.'
    }
}

function Get-RunMode {
    if ($Sweep) { 'SWEEP' } elseif ($Digest) { 'DIGEST' } elseif ($Change) { 'CHANGE' } else { 'APPLY' }
}

# Watchdog event base per mode: +0 clean, +1 with errors, +2 did no work.
# APPLY keeps 9100-9102, so anything already watching those carries on working.
function Get-EventBase {
    switch (Get-RunMode) { 'SWEEP' { 9110 } 'DIGEST' { 9120 } 'CHANGE' { 9130 } default { 9100 } }
}

# How an invocation ended, as the watchdog event and exit code it gets.
# Zero completed passes is NOT success: a run that never won the lock, or whose
# every pass threw, did nothing - and must not satisfy the Missing Event monitor.
function Get-RunOutcome {
    param([int]$Passes, [int]$Errors, [int]$LockSkips = 0)
    $base = Get-EventBase

    if ($Passes -le 0) {
        # No pass completed - but WHY decides which signal this is. Every
        # iteration deferring to the other task holding the mutex is a BUSY
        # system, not a broken one. Reporting that as x102 fires the did-no-work
        # rule on a healthy management server, and a slow SWEEP holding the lock
        # for longer than the APPLY budget does exactly that. x101 keeps the
        # Missing Event monitor satisfied - the script IS running and is not the
        # thing at fault - while still surfacing as a warning that names the cause.
        if ($LockSkips -gt 0) { return [pscustomobject]@{ EventId = $base + 1; EntryType = 'Warning';     ExitCode = 2 } }
        return                       [pscustomobject]@{ EventId = $base + 2; EntryType = 'Error';       ExitCode = 1 }
    }
    if ($Errors -gt 0) { return [pscustomobject]@{ EventId = $base + 1; EntryType = 'Warning';     ExitCode = 2 } }
    return                    [pscustomobject]@{ EventId = $base;     EntryType = 'Information'; ExitCode = 0 }
}

# The internal loop is an APPLY feature. SWEEP, DIGEST, CHANGE and -DryRun are
# always single-pass, whatever -LoopIntervalSeconds says. This used to THROW for
# them, so hard-coding LoopIntervalSeconds as the parameter default killed the
# SWEEP and DIGEST tasks outright. Now they simply ignore it - and only say so
# when it was passed on their own command line, where it is probably a mistake.
function Resolve-LoopSettings {
    param([System.Collections.IDictionary]$Bound = @{})

    $mode = Get-RunMode
    if ($script:LoopIntervalSeconds -gt 0 -and ($mode -ne 'APPLY' -or $DryRun)) {
        if ($Bound.ContainsKey('LoopIntervalSeconds')) {
            Write-Warning ("-LoopIntervalSeconds applies to APPLY only; {0} runs a single pass." -f
                           $(if ($DryRun) { '-DryRun' } else { $mode }))
        }
        $script:LoopIntervalSeconds = 0
    }
    if ($script:LoopIntervalSeconds -le 0) { return }

    if ($script:LoopIntervalSeconds -gt $LoopBudgetSeconds) {
        throw "LoopIntervalSeconds ($($script:LoopIntervalSeconds)) exceeds LoopBudgetSeconds ($LoopBudgetSeconds) - only one pass would ever run."
    }

    # The 90s default suits SWEEP waiting on an APPLY pass. Inside a 60s loop it
    # would let one slow sweep eat whole iterations, so derive it unless told.
    if (-not $Bound.ContainsKey('LockTimeoutSeconds')) {
        $script:LockTimeoutSeconds = [int][Math]::Max(1, [Math]::Min(20, [Math]::Floor($script:LoopIntervalSeconds / 3)))
    }
    elseif ($script:LockTimeoutSeconds -ge $script:LoopIntervalSeconds) {
        Write-Warning "LockTimeoutSeconds ($($script:LockTimeoutSeconds)) is not shorter than LoopIntervalSeconds ($($script:LoopIntervalSeconds)); a slow sweep will consume whole iterations waiting for the lock."
    }
}
Resolve-LoopSettings -Bound $PSBoundParameters

# The DNS/ping/TCP fan-out is only as parallel as the thread pool allows. On
# .NET Framework the pool grows roughly one thread per 500ms, so a 400-name
# batch serialises badly at the default minimum. Cheapest win in the script.
try { [System.Threading.ThreadPool]::SetMinThreads(256, 256) | Out-Null } catch { }

# ==========================================================================
# Logging
# ==========================================================================
function Invoke-LogRotation {
    if ($script:LogRotateChecked) { return }
    $script:LogRotateChecked = $true
    try {
        if (-not (Test-Path $LogPath)) { return }
        $fi = Get-Item -Path $LogPath -ErrorAction Stop
        if ($fi.Length -lt $LogMaxBytes) { return }

        for ($i = $LogKeep; $i -ge 1; $i--) {
            $older = "$LogPath.$i"
            $newer = if ($i -eq 1) { $LogPath } else { "$LogPath.$($i - 1)" }
            if (Test-Path $newer) {
                if ($i -eq $LogKeep -and (Test-Path $older)) { Remove-Item $older -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
                Move-Item -Path $newer -Destination $older -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
            }
        }
    } catch { }
}

function Write-Log {
    param([string]$Message, [string]$Level = 'INFO')
    if ($Level -eq 'ERROR') { $script:Errs++ }
    $line = '{0} [{1}] {2}' -f (Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss'), $Level, $Message
    Write-Verbose $line
    try {
        $dir = Split-Path -Path $LogPath -Parent
        if (-not (Test-Path $dir)) { New-Item -Path $dir -ItemType Directory -Force | Out-Null }
        Invoke-LogRotation
        Add-Content -Path $LogPath -Value $line
    } catch { }
}

# One event per invocation. A SCOM rule on the ABSENCE of the x100/x101 events is the
# watchdog - that is the one thing email can never tell you, because a script
# that has stopped running cannot send it.
function Write-AppEvent {
    param([int]$EventId, [string]$EntryType, [string]$Message)
    # A dry run must never write a watchdog event. The watchdog rule fires on their
    # ABSENCE, so a dry run that emitted one would quietly reassure you that a
    # script which changed nothing was doing its job.
    if ($NoEventLog -or $DryRun) { return }
    try {
        if ($null -eq $script:EventSourceOk) {
            $script:EventSourceOk = [System.Diagnostics.EventLog]::SourceExists($EventSource)
            if (-not $script:EventSourceOk) {
                # Needs elevation; succeeds once then never runs again.
                New-EventLog -LogName $EventLogName -Source $EventSource -ErrorAction Stop
                $script:EventSourceOk = $true
            }
        }
        if ($script:EventSourceOk) {
            Write-EventLog -LogName $EventLogName -Source $EventSource -EventId $EventId `
                -EntryType $EntryType -Message $Message -ErrorAction Stop
        }
    } catch {
        $script:EventSourceOk = $false
        Write-Verbose "Event log write failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"
    }
}

# One line of the dry-run plan. Goes to stdout so the scheduled task or the
# console shows it, and to the log so it can be compared with what a later real
# pass actually did.
function Write-Plan {
    param([string]$Action, [string]$Fqdn, [string]$Detail = '')
    $line = 'DRYRUN  {0,-11} {1,-45} {2}' -f $Action, $Fqdn, $Detail
    Write-Output $line
    Write-Log $line
}

# ==========================================================================
# Small helpers
# ==========================================================================
function ConvertTo-Utc {
    param([string]$Value)
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($Value)) { return $null }
    try {
        return [datetime]::Parse($Value, [Globalization.CultureInfo]::InvariantCulture,
                                 [Globalization.DateTimeStyles]::RoundtripKind).ToUniversalTime()
    } catch { return $null }
}

function ConvertTo-Int {
    param($Value)
    $n = 0
    if ($null -ne $Value) { [int]::TryParse([string]$Value, [ref]$n) | Out-Null }
    return $n
}

function ConvertTo-HashtableDeep {
    param($InputObject)
    if ($null -eq $InputObject) { return $null }
    if ($InputObject -is [System.Management.Automation.PSCustomObject]) {
        $h = @{}
        foreach ($p in $InputObject.PSObject.Properties) { $h[$p.Name] = ConvertTo-HashtableDeep $p.Value }
        return $h
    }
    if ($InputObject -is [System.Collections.IEnumerable] -and $InputObject -isnot [string]) {
        $l = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
        foreach ($i in $InputObject) { $l.Add((ConvertTo-HashtableDeep $i)) }
        return , $l.ToArray()
    }
    return $InputObject
}

function New-ParentDirectory {
    param([string]$Path)
    $dir = Split-Path -Path $Path -Parent
    if ($dir -and -not (Test-Path $dir)) { New-Item -Path $dir -ItemType Directory -Force | Out-Null }
}

# Proper CSV quoting for the hand-built event rows. An FQDN will never contain
# a comma, but a Detail field carrying an exception message can, and an
# unquoted comma silently shifts every later column on read-back.
function ConvertTo-CsvField {
    param($Value)
    $s = [string]$Value
    if ($s -match '[",\r\n]') { return '"' + ($s -replace '"', '""') + '"' }
    return $s
}

# ==========================================================================
# Exclusions - machines this script must never put into maintenance mode
# ==========================================================================
# Returns the entries as objects { Text; Pattern }. Re-read only when the file's
# timestamp changes, so an edit takes effect on the next pass with no restart
# and an unchanged file costs one Get-Item.
#
# FAILURE DIRECTION: a file that exists but cannot be read must never be taken
# as "nothing is excluded" - that is exactly how a management server ends up in
# maintenance mode. The last good copy is used if there is one; otherwise this
# throws, Invoke-Pass logs it as an ERROR, and the pass does nothing at all.
function Get-ExclusionList {
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($ExclusionFile)) { return }

    if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $ExclusionFile -PathType Leaf)) {
        if (-not $script:ExclusionWarned) {
            $script:ExclusionWarned = $true
            Write-Log "No exclusion file at $ExclusionFile - no machine is exempt from maintenance mode." 'WARN'
        }
        $script:Exclusions = @(); $script:ExclusionStamp = $null
        return
    }

    try {
        $stamp = (Get-Item -LiteralPath $ExclusionFile -ErrorAction Stop).LastWriteTimeUtc
        if ($null -ne $script:Exclusions -and $script:ExclusionStamp -eq $stamp) { return $script:Exclusions }

        $list = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
        foreach ($line in @(Get-Content -LiteralPath $ExclusionFile -ErrorAction Stop)) {
            $text = ([string]$line -replace '#.*$', '').Trim()
            if (-not $text) { continue }

            $wp = New-Object System.Management.Automation.WildcardPattern($text, [System.Management.Automation.WildcardOptions]::IgnoreCase)
            try { [void]$wp.IsMatch('') }
            catch {
                # e.g. an unbalanced '['. Match it literally rather than drop it.
                Write-Log "Exclusion entry '$text' is not a valid wildcard pattern - matching it literally." 'WARN'
                $wp = New-Object System.Management.Automation.WildcardPattern(
                          [System.Management.Automation.WildcardPattern]::Escape($text),
                          [System.Management.Automation.WildcardOptions]::IgnoreCase)
            }
            $list.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Text = $text; Pattern = $wp })
        }

        $script:Exclusions     = $list.ToArray()
        $script:ExclusionStamp = $stamp
        Write-Log ("Exclusion list loaded from {0}: {1} entr{2}{3}" -f $ExclusionFile, $list.Count,
                   $(if ($list.Count -eq 1) { 'y' } else { 'ies' }),
                   $(if ($list.Count -gt 0) { ' - ' + (($list | ForEach-Object { $_.Text }) -join ', ') } else { '' }))
        return $script:Exclusions
    } catch {
        if ($null -ne $script:Exclusions -and $null -ne $script:ExclusionStamp) {
            Write-Log "Exclusion file $ExclusionFile could not be re-read ($($_.Exception.Message)); using the copy loaded earlier." 'WARN'
            return $script:Exclusions
        }
        throw "Exclusion file $ExclusionFile exists but cannot be read ($($_.Exception.Message)). Nothing is done this pass rather than risk putting an excluded machine into maintenance mode."
    }
}

# The exclusion entry a machine matches, or $null. Tested against the FQDN and
# the short host name, so 'SCOMMS01' and 'scomms01.contoso.com' both match.
function Get-ExclusionMatch {
    param([string]$Fqdn, [object[]]$Exclusions)
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($Fqdn) -or -not $Exclusions) { return $null }
    $short = $Fqdn.Split('.')[0]
    foreach ($x in $Exclusions) {
        if ($x.Pattern.IsMatch($Fqdn) -or $x.Pattern.IsMatch($short)) { return $x.Text }
    }
    return $null
}

# ==========================================================================
# Tracked state (the machines currently suppressed)
# ==========================================================================
# Reads the old schema too - missing columns default rather than blowing up.
# Sets $script:StateFileExisted, which is how state LOSS is told apart from a
# legitimate drain to zero.
function Get-TrackedState {
    $script:StateRecoveredFrom = $null
    $script:StateFileExisted   = Test-Path $StateFile

    $rows = $null
    if ($script:StateFileExisted) {
        try {
            $rows = @(Import-Csv -Path $StateFile -ErrorAction Stop)
        } catch {
            # An unreadable file is loss, not emptiness. Say so and do not
            # silently start from scratch.
            Write-Log "State file exists but could not be parsed: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
            $rows = $null
            $script:StateFileExisted = $false

            # Keep the unreadable file. Recovery below would otherwise overwrite
            # the only evidence of how it got corrupted.
            if (-not $DryRun) {
                try {
                    Move-Item -Path $StateFile -Destination "$StateFile.corrupt" -Force
                    Write-Log "Unreadable state file kept at ${StateFile}.corrupt for inspection." 'WARN'
                } catch {
                    Write-Log "Could not set the unreadable state file aside: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # ---- Recover from the backup ------------------------------------------
    # Set-TrackedState leaves the previous generation at .bak on every
    # successful write, so it is at most one pass behind. Reading it beats
    # starting from nothing by a wide margin: every machine it names really is
    # in maintenance mode right now, and without this they would stay suppressed
    # and untracked until their windows expired - which at a weekend peak is a
    # few hundred machines going dark and then alerting together.
    #
    # What is lost is at most one pass of changes, and the next sweep re-derives
    # all of it from SCOM anyway.
    if ($null -eq $rows) {
        $bak = "$StateFile.bak"
        if (Test-Path $bak) {
            try {
                $bakRows = @(Import-Csv -Path $bak -ErrorAction Stop)
                if ($bakRows.Count -gt 0) {
                    $rows = $bakRows
                    $script:StateRecoveredFrom = $bak
                    $script:StateFileExisted   = $true
                    Write-Log "Primary state file missing or unreadable - recovered $($bakRows.Count) machine(s) from $bak." 'ERROR'
                    Add-EventRecord -Type 'StateRecovered' -Fqdn '' -Detail $bakRows.Count

                    if (-not $DryRun) {
                        # Put the primary back, so this is a one-off event rather
                        # than something every pass repeats.
                        try { Copy-Item -Path $bak -Destination $StateFile -Force }
                        catch { Write-Log "Recovered in memory, but could not restore the primary state file: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN' }
                    }
                } else {
                    # An empty backup recovers nothing. Do not dress that up as
                    # a recovery - fall through to the loss path.
                    Write-Log "Backup state file at $bak is empty; nothing to recover." 'WARN'
                }
            } catch {
                Write-Log "Backup state file at $bak is also unreadable: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
            }
        }
    }

    if ($null -eq $rows) { return @() }

    $out  = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    $seen = @{}
    foreach ($r in $rows) {
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($r.Fqdn) -or [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($r.InstanceId)) { continue }
        if ($seen[$r.InstanceId]) { continue }   # tolerate a duplicated file
        $seen[$r.InstanceId] = $true
        $props = $r.PSObject.Properties
        $out.Add([pscustomobject]@{
            Fqdn            = $r.Fqdn
            InstanceId      = $r.InstanceId
            AppliedUtc      = $r.AppliedUtc
            EndUtc          = $r.EndUtc
            PingStreak      = $(if ($props['PingStreak'])      { ConvertTo-Int $r.PingStreak }     else { 0 })
            PingOnlyStreak  = $(if ($props['PingOnlyStreak'])  { ConvertTo-Int $r.PingOnlyStreak } else { 0 })
            LastReportedUtc = $(if ($props['LastReportedUtc']) { $r.LastReportedUtc }              else { '' })
            LastDnsOkUtc    = $(if ($props['LastDnsOkUtc'])    { $r.LastDnsOkUtc }                 else { '' })
            ForeignSinceUtc = $(if ($props['ForeignSinceUtc']) { $r.ForeignSinceUtc }              else { '' })
        })
    }
    return $out.ToArray()
}

function Set-TrackedState {
    param($Rows)
    if ($DryRun) { Write-Verbose 'DRYRUN: tracked state not written.'; return }
    New-ParentDirectory -Path $StateFile
    $arr = [object[]]$Rows # $arr    = @($Rows)
    $header = 'Fqdn,InstanceId,AppliedUtc,EndUtc,PingStreak,PingOnlyStreak,LastReportedUtc,LastDnsOkUtc,ForeignSinceUtc'

    # Write beside the target and swap, so a crash mid-write cannot leave a
    # truncated state file - that would strand everything in it.
    $tmp = "$StateFile.tmp"
    if ($arr.Count -gt 0) {
        $arr | Select-Object Fqdn, InstanceId, AppliedUtc, EndUtc, PingStreak, PingOnlyStreak, LastReportedUtc, LastDnsOkUtc, ForeignSinceUtc |
            Export-Csv -Path $tmp -NoTypeInformation -Force
    } else {
        Set-Content -Path $tmp -Value $header -Force
    }

    if (Test-Path $StateFile) {
        # Replace is atomic and leaves the previous generation as .bak.
        try {
            [System.IO.File]::Replace($tmp, $StateFile, "$StateFile.bak", $true)
        } catch {
            # Replace refused (a locked .bak, antivirus, an odd filesystem). The
            # plain move used to skip the backup, so the next loss of the
            # primary had nothing - or something stale - to recover from.
            # Not atomic, but it keeps the one-pass-behind promise.
            Write-Log "State file swap failed ($($_.Exception.Message)); falling back to copy-then-move." 'WARN'
            try { Copy-Item -Path $StateFile -Destination "$StateFile.bak" -Force -ErrorAction Stop }
            catch { Write-Log "Could not refresh ${StateFile}.bak: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN' }
            Move-Item -Path $tmp -Destination $StateFile -Force
        }
    } else {
        Move-Item -Path $tmp -Destination $StateFile -Force
    }
}

# ==========================================================================
# Event history (feeds the weekly digest)
# ==========================================================================
function Add-EventRecord {
    param([string]$Type, [string]$Fqdn, [string]$Detail = '')
    if ($DryRun) { return }
    try {
        New-ParentDirectory -Path $EventCsvPath
        if (-not (Test-Path $EventCsvPath)) {
            Set-Content -Path $EventCsvPath -Value 'Utc,Type,Fqdn,Detail' -Force
        }
        $line = @(
            ConvertTo-CsvField ([datetime]::UtcNow.ToString('o'))
            ConvertTo-CsvField $Type
            ConvertTo-CsvField $Fqdn
            ConvertTo-CsvField $Detail
        ) -join ','
        Add-Content -Path $EventCsvPath -Value $line
    } catch {
        Write-Verbose "Event record write failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"
    }
}

function Get-EventRecords {
    param([int]$Days = 7)
    if (-not (Test-Path $EventCsvPath)) { return @() }
    $cutoff = [datetime]::UtcNow.AddDays(-$Days)
    try {
        return @(Import-Csv -Path $EventCsvPath -ErrorAction Stop | Where-Object {
            $t = ConvertTo-Utc $_.Utc
            $t -and $t -ge $cutoff
        })
    } catch {
        Write-Log "Event history unreadable: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        return @()
    }
}

# ==========================================================================
# Notification state (rate limiting, and the counters that survive state loss)
# ==========================================================================
# The notification-state file THIS invocation owns. CHANGE keeps its own, so the
# two mutexes never share a read-modify-write file. See ChangeNotifyStateFile.
#
# Resolved on every call rather than latched at startup, so anything that sets
# $NotifyStateFile after the parameter block - the test harness does exactly
# this to redirect state into a temp directory - still redirects it.
function Get-NotifyPath {
    if ($Change) { return $ChangeNotifyStateFile }
    return $NotifyStateFile
}

function Get-NotifyState {
    $empty = @{ LastTrackedCount = 0; Orphans = @{}; Excluded = @{}; Notify = @{} }
    $path = Get-NotifyPath
    if (-not (Test-Path $path)) { return $empty }
    try {
        $raw = Get-Content -Path $path -Raw -ErrorAction Stop
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($raw)) { return $empty }
        $h = ConvertTo-HashtableDeep ($raw | ConvertFrom-Json)
        if ($null -eq $h)                  { return $empty }
        if ($null -eq $h['Orphans'])       { $h['Orphans'] = @{} }
        if ($null -eq $h['Excluded'])      { $h['Excluded'] = @{} }
        if ($null -eq $h['Notify'])        { $h['Notify'] = @{} }
        if ($null -eq $h['LastTrackedCount']) { $h['LastTrackedCount'] = 0 }
        return $h
    } catch {
        Write-Log "Notification state unreadable, starting fresh: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        return $empty
    }
}

function Set-NotifyState {
    param([hashtable]$State)
    if ($DryRun) { Write-Verbose 'DRYRUN: notification state not written.'; return }
    try {
        $path = Get-NotifyPath
        New-ParentDirectory -Path $path
        # Per-PROCESS temp name. Two invocations both writing "<file>.tmp" clobber
        # each other's temp, and one Move-Item then fails into the catch below -
        # silently losing that update.
        $tmp = "$path.$PID.tmp"
        ($State | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6) | Set-Content -Path $tmp -Force -Encoding UTF8
        Move-Item -Path $tmp -Destination $path -Force
    } catch {
        Write-Log "Could not write notification state: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
    }
}

# ==========================================================================
# Mail
# ==========================================================================
function Test-MailConfigured {
    $ok = -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($SmtpServer) -and
          -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($MailFrom) -and
          $MailTo.Count -gt 0
    if (-not $ok -and -not $script:MailWarned) {
        $script:MailWarned = $true
        Write-Verbose 'Mail is not configured (SmtpServer / MailFrom / MailTo); notifications will be logged only.'
    }
    return $ok
}

function Get-SmtpCredential {
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($SmtpCredentialPath)) { return $null }
    if ($script:SmtpCred) { return $script:SmtpCred }
    try {
        $script:SmtpCred = Import-Clixml -Path $SmtpCredentialPath -ErrorAction Stop
        return $script:SmtpCred
    } catch {
        # DPAPI is bound to the exporting account AND machine.
        Write-Log "SMTP credential at '$SmtpCredentialPath' could not be read - re-export it as the task's service account on this machine: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
        return $null
    }
}

# Never throws. A failed send must never cost a suppression.
function Send-Mail {
    param(
        [string[]]$To,
        [string]$Subject,
        [string]$Body,
        [string[]]$Attachment = @()
    )
    if ($DryRun) { Write-Log "DRYRUN: would mail '$Subject' to $($To -join ', ')."; return $true }
    $smtp = $null; $msg = $null
    $opened = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    try {
        $smtp           = New-Object System.Net.Mail.SmtpClient($SmtpServer, $SmtpPort)
        $smtp.Timeout   = $SmtpTimeoutMs
        $smtp.EnableSsl = [bool]$SmtpUseSsl

        $cred = Get-SmtpCredential
        if ($cred) {
            $nc = $cred.GetNetworkCredential()
            $smtp.Credentials = New-Object System.Net.NetworkCredential($nc.UserName, $nc.Password, $nc.Domain)
        }

        $msg         = New-Object System.Net.Mail.MailMessage
        $msg.From    = New-Object System.Net.Mail.MailAddress($MailFrom)
        $msg.Subject = $Subject
        $msg.Body    = $Body
        foreach ($t in $To) { if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($t)) { $msg.To.Add($t.Trim()) } }
        if ($msg.To.Count -eq 0) { return $false }

        foreach ($a in $Attachment) {
            if ($a -and (Test-Path $a)) {
                $att = New-Object System.Net.Mail.Attachment($a)
                $opened.Add($att)
                $msg.Attachments.Add($att)
            }
        }

        $smtp.Send($msg)
        return $true
    } catch {
        Write-Log "Mail send failed: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        return $false
    } finally {
        foreach ($a in $opened) { try { $a.Dispose() } catch { } }
        if ($msg)  { try { $msg.Dispose() }  catch { } }
        if ($smtp) { try { $smtp.Dispose() } catch { } }
    }
}

# Rate-limited notification. $Key identifies the notification type; the same
# key cannot send more often than $MinIntervalMinutes. Suppressed occurrences
# are counted and reported in the next message that does go out, so a quiet
# mailbox never means "nothing happened".
function Send-Notification {
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$Key,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$Subject,
        [Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$Body,
        [int]$MinIntervalMinutes = 60,
        [ValidateSet('INFO', 'WARN', 'ERROR')][string]$Severity = 'WARN',
        [string[]]$To = @(),
        [string[]]$Attachment = @()
    )

    Write-Log "NOTIFY[$Key] $Subject" $Severity

    if ($DryRun) { Write-Plan 'NOTIFY' $Key $Subject; return }
    if (-not (Test-MailConfigured)) { return }

    $recipients = if ($To.Count -gt 0) { $To } else { $MailTo }
    $nowUtc     = [datetime]::UtcNow

    $state = Get-NotifyState
    $entry = $state['Notify'][$Key]
    if ($null -eq $entry) {
        $entry = @{ NextEligibleUtc = ''; LastSentUtc = ''; SuppressedCount = 0; FirstSuppressedUtc = '' }
    }

    $nextEligible = ConvertTo-Utc ([string]$entry['NextEligibleUtc'])
    if ($nextEligible -and $nowUtc -lt $nextEligible) {
        $entry['SuppressedCount'] = (ConvertTo-Int $entry['SuppressedCount']) + 1
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$entry['FirstSuppressedUtc'])) {
            $entry['FirstSuppressedUtc'] = $nowUtc.ToString('o')
        }
        $state['Notify'][$Key] = $entry
        Set-NotifyState -State $state
        Write-Verbose "Notification '$Key' suppressed (next eligible $nextEligible UTC)."
        return
    }

    $suppressed = ConvertTo-Int $entry['SuppressedCount']
    $preamble   = ''
    if ($suppressed -gt 0) {
        $since = ConvertTo-Utc ([string]$entry['FirstSuppressedUtc'])
        $preamble = ("NOTE: {0} further occurrence(s) of this condition were suppressed by rate limiting{1}.`r`n`r`n" -f
                     $suppressed, $(if ($since) { " since $($since.ToString('u'))" } else { '' }))
    }

    $full = @(
        $preamble
        $Body
        ''
        '---'
        ("Host            : {0}" -f $env:COMPUTERNAME)
        ("Management group: {0}" -f ($ManagementServer -join ', '))
        ("Generated       : {0} UTC" -f $nowUtc.ToString('u'))
        ("Notification    : {0} (rate limit {1} min)" -f $Key, $MinIntervalMinutes)
        ("Log             : {0}" -f $LogPath)
    ) -join "`r`n"

    $subj = '{0}[{1}] {2}' -f $MailSubjectPrefix, $Severity, $Subject
    $sent = Send-Mail -To $recipients -Subject $subj -Body $full -Attachment $Attachment

    if ($sent) {
        $entry['LastSentUtc']        = $nowUtc.ToString('o')
        $entry['NextEligibleUtc']    = $nowUtc.AddMinutes($MinIntervalMinutes).ToString('o')
        $entry['SuppressedCount']    = 0
        $entry['FirstSuppressedUtc'] = ''
    } else {
        # Back off briefly rather than retrying every pass into a dead gateway,
        # but do not burn the whole interval on a transient failure.
        $entry['NextEligibleUtc'] = $nowUtc.AddMinutes($FailureRetryMinutes).ToString('o')
    }

    $state['Notify'][$Key] = $entry
    Set-NotifyState -State $state
}

# ==========================================================================
# SCOM helpers
# ==========================================================================
# One SDK round trip per 200 machines instead of one per machine. Returns a
# hashtable keyed by instance id string.
function Get-InstanceMap {
    param([string[]]$Ids)
    $map = @{}
    if (-not $Ids -or $Ids.Count -eq 0) { return $map }

    $guids = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[guid]
    foreach ($id in $Ids) {
        $g = [guid]::Empty
        if ([guid]::TryParse([string]$id, [ref]$g)) { $guids.Add($g) }
    }
    if ($guids.Count -eq 0) { return $map }

    $chunk = 200
    for ($i = 0; $i -lt $guids.Count; $i += $chunk) {
        $slice = $guids.GetRange($i, [Math]::Min($chunk, $guids.Count - $i)).ToArray()
        try {
            foreach ($inst in @(Get-SCOMClassInstance -Id $slice)) {
                if ($inst) { $map[$inst.Id.ToString()] = $inst }
            }
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Instance fetch failed for a chunk of $($slice.Count): $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
        }
    }
    return $map
}

# The read that decides whether an MM write took. Same as Get-InstanceMap, but
# anything that does not read as expected gets a settle wait and up to two more
# looks before it is called a failure.
#
# "The SDK call did not throw" is not proof, which is why every write here is
# verified - but the verification read used to happen on the same tick as the
# write. A read that catches the pre-write state gets logged as a hard MM
# failure, counted in Stats.MmFailures, and at MmFailureAlertCount of them in
# one pass it mails "maintenance mode failing wholesale" on a pass where nothing
# was wrong. Monday morning, releasing a hundred machines at once, is precisely
# where that threshold gets crossed.
#
# Only the stragglers are re-read, AND the settle wait is only paid when
# something actually reads stale - so the normal case, everything took, now
# costs one read and no sleep at all. Every millisecond in here is spent holding
# the shutdown mutex, and a sweep that holds it longer than the whole APPLY
# budget starves APPLY for an entire invocation. The worst case is unchanged.
function Get-VerifiedInstanceMap {
    param(
        [string[]]$Ids,
        [bool]$ExpectInMaintenanceMode
    )
    if (-not $Ids -or $Ids.Count -eq 0) { return @{} }

    # Read first. The SDK round trip is usually settle enough on its own.
    $map = Get-InstanceMap -Ids $Ids
    $unsettled = @($Ids | Where-Object {
        $i = $map[[string]$_]
        (-not $i) -or ($i.InMaintenanceMode -ne $ExpectInMaintenanceMode)
    })
    if ($unsettled.Count -eq 0) { return $map }

    if ($VerifySettleMs -gt 0) {
        Write-Verbose "$($unsettled.Count) instance(s) had not settled; re-reading after $VerifySettleMs ms."
        Start-Sleep -Milliseconds $VerifySettleMs
        foreach ($kv in (Get-InstanceMap -Ids $unsettled).GetEnumerator()) { $map[$kv.Key] = $kv.Value }
        $unsettled = @($unsettled | Where-Object {
            $i = $map[[string]$_]
            (-not $i) -or ($i.InMaintenanceMode -ne $ExpectInMaintenanceMode)
        })
        if ($unsettled.Count -eq 0) { return $map }
    }

    if ($VerifyRetryMs -le 0) { return $map }

    Write-Verbose "$($unsettled.Count) instance(s) still unsettled; one more look after $VerifyRetryMs ms."
    Start-Sleep -Milliseconds $VerifyRetryMs
    foreach ($kv in (Get-InstanceMap -Ids $unsettled).GetEnumerator()) { $map[$kv.Key] = $kv.Value }
    return $map
}

# Resolve DNS once, then ping and TCP-probe the resolved address concurrently.
#
# Ping.SendPingAsync(hostname, ...) resolves DNS on the CALLING thread on
# .NET Framework, so a batch full of stale names serialises into a queue of DNS
# timeouts - exactly the batch this script accumulates over multi-week outages.
# Resolving up front also lets ping and the port probe share one lookup.
#
# Returns a hashtable keyed by the name passed in:
#   Resolved  - DNS answered
#   Ping      - ICMP echo succeeded
#   Agent     - TCP connect to $AgentPort succeeded (HealthService is listening)
#   TcpStatus - why Agent reads the way it does: Connected, TimedOut, a
#               SocketErrorCode such as ConnectionRefused, or NotProbed.
#               Diagnostic only - nothing decides anything on it. A dead agent
#               on a live machine refuses the connection; a firewall drops it
#               and the probe times out. Both read Agent = $false, and telling
#               them apart is the difference between chasing HealthService and
#               chasing a firewall change.
# Public entry point. Slices the work into waves of $ProbeBatchSize so the
# number of sockets and ping handles held open at once is bounded, however many
# machines are tracked. Time cost is one slow probe per wave, not per machine.
function Test-AgentReachability {
    param([string[]]$ComputerNames)

    $result = @{}
    if (-not $ComputerNames -or $ComputerNames.Count -eq 0) { return $result }

    # Timed, because this is the one part of the sweep whose cost can run away
    # silently. It should be roughly (waves x (PingTimeoutMs + TcpTimeoutMs))
    # and nothing like the machine count. If this line starts reporting minutes,
    # the per-protocol deadline in the wave below has been broken.
    $sw    = [System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch]::StartNew()
    $names = @($ComputerNames | Select-Object -Unique)
    for ($i = 0; $i -lt $names.Count; $i += $ProbeBatchSize) {
        $slice = @($names[$i..([Math]::Min($i + $ProbeBatchSize, $names.Count) - 1)])
        $wave  = Test-AgentReachabilityWave -ComputerNames $slice
        foreach ($kv in $wave.GetEnumerator()) { $result[$kv.Key] = $kv.Value }
    }
    $sw.Stop()

    # The TCP breakdown is what makes the PortBlind warning below actionable:
    # a wave that is all ConnectionRefused is a fleet of dead agents on live
    # machines, and a wave that is all TimedOut is the network.
    $byStatus = @{}
    foreach ($v in $result.Values) {
        $k = [string]$v.TcpStatus
        if (-not $byStatus.ContainsKey($k)) { $byStatus[$k] = 0 }
        $byStatus[$k]++
    }
    $breakdown = ((@($byStatus.GetEnumerator() | Sort-Object -Property Name) |
                   ForEach-Object { '{0}={1}' -f $_.Key, $_.Value }) -join ' ')

    Write-Log ('Probed {0} machine(s) in {1} wave(s) in {2:N1}s. TCP {3}: {4}' -f
               $names.Count, [Math]::Ceiling($names.Count / [double]$ProbeBatchSize), $sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds,
               $AgentPort, $breakdown)
    return $result
}

function Test-AgentReachabilityWave {
    param([string[]]$ComputerNames)

    $result = @{}
    if (-not $ComputerNames -or $ComputerNames.Count -eq 0) { return $result }

    foreach ($n in $ComputerNames) {
        # Unresolvable / unreachable = down. Never guess up.
        $result[$n] = [pscustomobject]@{ Resolved = $false; Ping = $false; Agent = $false; TcpStatus = 'NotProbed' }
    }

    # --- DNS, all in flight at once ----------------------------------------
    $dns = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    foreach ($name in @($ComputerNames | Select-Object -Unique)) {
        try {
            $dns.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Name = $name; Task = [System.Net.Dns]::GetHostAddressesAsync($name) })
        } catch { }
    }

    $targets = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    foreach ($d in $dns) {
        try {
            if ($d.Task.Wait($DnsTimeoutMs)) {
                $addr = @($d.Task.Result | Where-Object { $_.AddressFamily -eq 'InterNetwork' }) | Select-Object -First 1
                if (-not $addr) { $addr = @($d.Task.Result) | Select-Object -First 1 }
                if ($addr) {
                    $result[$d.Name].Resolved = $true
                    $targets.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Name = $d.Name; Address = $addr })
                }
            }
        } catch { }
    }
    if ($targets.Count -eq 0) { return $result }

    # --- ping + TCP, both in flight ----------------------------------------
    $pending = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    foreach ($t in $targets) {
        $ping = $null; $pTask = $null; $tcp = $null; $tTask = $null

        try {
            $ping  = New-Object System.Net.NetworkInformation.Ping
            $pTask = $ping.SendPingAsync($t.Address, $PingTimeoutMs)
        } catch {
            if ($ping) { try { $ping.Dispose() } catch { } }
            $ping = $null; $pTask = $null
        }

        if (-not $SkipAgentPortCheck) {
            try {
                $tcp   = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient
                $tTask = $tcp.ConnectAsync($t.Address, $AgentPort)
            } catch {
                if ($tcp) { try { $tcp.Close() } catch { } }
                $tcp = $null; $tTask = $null
            }
        }

        $pending.Add([pscustomobject]@{
            Name = $t.Name; Ping = $ping; PingTask = $pTask; Tcp = $tcp; TcpTask = $tTask
        })
    }

    # ONE deadline per protocol for the whole wave, not one timeout per machine.
    #
    # Every task above was started before this point, so they are all running
    # concurrently - but giving each WAIT its own fresh timeout makes the waits
    # sequential for anything that does not complete. Ping got away with it:
    # SendPingAsync gives up on its own at $PingTimeoutMs, so those tasks really
    # have finished by the time this loop reaches them. ConnectAsync has no
    # timeout of its own. Against a switched-off host on a network that does not
    # answer with ICMP unreachable, the connect sits in SYN retransmit for ~21s,
    # so every per-task wait burned its full $TcpTimeoutMs and they ADDED UP.
    #
    # That is this script's whole population - machines deliberately down but
    # still holding a DNS record - so a wave of 250 cost up to 250 x
    # $TcpTimeoutMs instead of $TcpTimeoutMs. At 3s that is 12.5 minutes for one
    # wave: the sweep overruns its own schedule, the 9100 watchdog event goes
    # quiet long enough to fire the absence rule, and nothing else can win the
    # lock. Anchoring both loops to an absolute deadline is the whole fix.
    $pingDeadline = [datetime]::UtcNow.AddMilliseconds($PingTimeoutMs + 1500)
    $tcpDeadline  = [datetime]::UtcNow.AddMilliseconds($TcpTimeoutMs)

    foreach ($p in $pending) {
        if ($p.PingTask) {
            try {
                $left = [int][Math]::Max(0, ($pingDeadline - [datetime]::UtcNow).TotalMilliseconds)
                if ($left -gt 0) { [void][System.Threading.Tasks.Task]::WaitAny(@($p.PingTask), $left) }
                # Status is checked first and -and short-circuits, so .Result is
                # only touched on a task that has actually completed - reading it
                # on a pending task would block with no timeout at all.
                $result[$p.Name].Ping = ($p.PingTask.Status -eq 'RanToCompletion' -and
                                         $p.PingTask.Result.Status -eq 'Success')
            } catch {
            } finally { try { $p.Ping.Dispose() } catch { } }
        }

        if ($p.TcpTask) {
            # WaitAny, not Wait: a refused connection FAULTS the task and
            # Wait() would rethrow it. A refusal is a definitive "not
            # listening", not an error worth reporting.
            try {
                $left = [int][Math]::Max(0, ($tcpDeadline - [datetime]::UtcNow).TotalMilliseconds)
                if ($left -gt 0) { [void][System.Threading.Tasks.Task]::WaitAny(@($p.TcpTask), $left) }
                $result[$p.Name].Agent = ($p.TcpTask.Status -eq 'RanToCompletion')

                # Record WHY, not just whether. Reading .Exception also observes
                # the fault, which the sweep at the end of this function does
                # anyway. A task still pending at the deadline is a timeout.
                $result[$p.Name].TcpStatus = $(
                    if ($p.TcpTask.Status -eq 'RanToCompletion') { 'Connected' }
                    elseif ($p.TcpTask.IsFaulted) {
                        $se = @($p.TcpTask.Exception.InnerExceptions |
                                Where-Object { $_ -is [System.Net.Sockets.SocketException] }) | Select-Object -First 1
                        if ($se) { [string]$se.SocketErrorCode } else { 'Failed' }
                    }
                    else { 'TimedOut' }
                )
            } catch {
            } finally {
                # Close() aborts a connect still in SYN retry, so no socket is
                # left held once the wave is done with it.
                try { $p.Tcp.Close() } catch { }
            }
        }
    }

    # Observe any connect that faulted. Close() aborts asynchronously, so a
    # fault can land after the loop above has moved on - checking inside that
    # finally missed most of them. Unobserved task exceptions are harmless on
    # .NET 4.5+, but there is no reason to leave them for the finalizer.
    foreach ($p in $pending) {
        if ($p.TcpTask -and $p.TcpTask.IsFaulted) { $null = $p.TcpTask.Exception }
    }

    return $result
}

function Resolve-WindowsComputer {
    param([string]$Fqdn)
    $candidates = @(Get-SCOMClassInstance -Name $Fqdn -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue) |
                  Where-Object { $_.DisplayName -ieq $Fqdn }
    if (-not $candidates) { return $null }

    # VERIFY (2): FullName is '<least derived non-abstract class>:<key>'.
    $c = $candidates | Where-Object { $_.FullName -like 'Microsoft.Windows.Computer:*' } | Select-Object -First 1
    if ($c) { return $c }

    # Fallback: a Windows Computer sits at the top of its hosting chain, so its
    # Path is empty; hosted objects sharing the display name carry the computer
    # FQDN as their Path. Less precise - other unhosted computer classes match too.
    return ($candidates | Where-Object { [string]::IsNullOrEmpty($_.Path) } | Select-Object -First 1)
}

# Works out how this SDK build reports a window end carrying an Unspecified
# DateTimeKind, by comparing one against a window WE asked for and therefore
# already know the answer to.
#
# Guessing is not safe here, and the old code guessed UTC. If the build actually
# hands back local time, that is wrong by the server's UTC offset - ten hours in
# Australia - and it is wrong in the direction that hides itself: the recorded
# EndUtc lands FURTHER in the future than the real window, so no extend is ever
# scheduled, the real window lapses, the machine alerts, and the next sweep
# quietly reapplies. Nothing in the log says why it keeps happening.
#
# Only ever called with an end time this script requested, so a match is proof
# rather than inference. It latches once per invocation.
function Register-MaintenanceWindowKind {
    param([datetime]$Raw, [datetime]$RequestedEndUtc)
    if ($script:MmWindowKind) { return }
    if ($Raw.Kind -ne [System.DateTimeKind]::Unspecified) { return }

    $asUtc   = [datetime]::SpecifyKind($Raw, [System.DateTimeKind]::Utc)
    $asLocal = [datetime]::SpecifyKind($Raw, [System.DateTimeKind]::Local).ToUniversalTime()
    $dUtc    = [Math]::Abs(($asUtc   - $RequestedEndUtc).TotalMinutes)
    $dLocal  = [Math]::Abs(($asLocal - $RequestedEndUtc).TotalMinutes)

    # Neither reading lands near what we asked for, so this is not the window we
    # set - a failed extend, or somebody amended it. Says nothing about kind.
    if ([Math]::Min($dUtc, $dLocal) -gt 10) { return }

    # The two readings agree, so the server runs on UTC and the distinction does
    # not exist here. Latch anyway, to stop re-testing every sweep.
    if ([Math]::Abs($dUtc - $dLocal) -lt 1) { $script:MmWindowKind = 'Utc'; return }

    $script:MmWindowKind = $(if ($dLocal -lt $dUtc) { 'Local' } else { 'Utc' })
    Write-Log ("Maintenance window end times come back with an unspecified DateTimeKind; calibrated against a window this script set as {0} time." -f
               $script:MmWindowKind)
}

# Current maintenance window end, in UTC, or $null if it cannot be established.
# $null means "cannot confirm" and must never be read as "not extended".
#
# CalibrateAgainstUtc is the end time we just asked SCOM for, when the caller
# has one. It is used only to settle the Unspecified-kind question above.
function Get-MaintenanceEndUtc {
    param($Instance, [datetime]$CalibrateAgainstUtc = [datetime]::MinValue)
    if (-not $Instance -or -not $Instance.InMaintenanceMode) { return $null }
    if ($script:MmWindowReadable -eq $false) { return $null }

    $why = $null
    try {
        $w = $Instance.GetMaintenanceWindow()
        if ($null -eq $w) {
            $why = 'GetMaintenanceWindow() returned nothing for an object that is in maintenance mode'
        } else {
            $e = $w.ScheduledEndTime
            if ($e -is [datetime]) {
                $script:MmWindowReadable = $true
                if ($e.Kind -eq [System.DateTimeKind]::Local) { return $e.ToUniversalTime() }
                if ($e.Kind -eq [System.DateTimeKind]::Utc)   { return $e }

                # Unspecified. Settle it if the caller handed us a window of our
                # own to compare against.
                if ($CalibrateAgainstUtc -gt [datetime]::MinValue) {
                    Register-MaintenanceWindowKind -Raw $e -RequestedEndUtc $CalibrateAgainstUtc
                }
                if ($script:MmWindowKind -eq 'Local') {
                    return [datetime]::SpecifyKind($e, [System.DateTimeKind]::Local).ToUniversalTime()
                }
                # Uncalibrated: assume UTC, exactly as this did unconditionally
                # before - but say so once, so an unexplained reapply cycle has
                # something in the log to point at.
                if (-not $script:MmWindowKindWarned) {
                    $script:MmWindowKindWarned = $true
                    Write-Log ("Maintenance window end times carry an unspecified DateTimeKind (first seen {0:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}); treating them as UTC until a window this script set can settle it." -f $e) 'WARN'
                }
                return [datetime]::SpecifyKind($e, [System.DateTimeKind]::Utc)
            }
            $why = 'ScheduledEndTime is not a DateTime on this SDK build'
        }
    } catch {
        $why = $_.Exception.Message
    }

    # Degrade to the old behaviour - confirm by InMaintenanceMode alone - rather
    # than churning an extend every sweep for a window we simply cannot read.
    if ($null -eq $script:MmWindowReadable) {
        $script:MmWindowReadable = $false
        Write-Log "Maintenance window end times are not readable here ($why); extends will be confirmed by InMaintenanceMode alone." 'WARN'
    }
    return $null
}

# Who set the maintenance window this object is in: 'Ours', 'Foreign', or
# 'Unknown'. Decided by looking for $Marker in the window comment.
#
# THE FAILURE DIRECTION IS DELIBERATE. Anything we cannot positively read as
# somebody else's comes back 'Unknown', and every caller treats 'Unknown'
# exactly as it treated the world before ownership existed - it extends and it
# releases. If this returned 'Foreign' on an unreadable comment, an SDK build
# that does not surface window comments would stop the script extending or
# releasing ANY machine: the tracked population would climb to TrackedCeiling
# and the estate would go blind, which is the precise runaway the ceiling guard
# exists to catch. Under-detecting costs an administrator an argument. The other
# way round costs the monitoring.
function Get-MaintenanceOwner {
    param($Instance)
    if (-not $Instance -or -not $Instance.InMaintenanceMode) { return 'Unknown' }

    # A window put there by this script's own CHANGE mode is foreign to the
    # shutdown side: never extended, never released, and a machine that comes
    # back is dropped from tracking with the window left in place. Decided from
    # our own state file rather than the window comment, so it holds even on an
    # SDK build where comments cannot be read - which is exactly the case where
    # the Unknown fallback below would otherwise claim the window as ours.
    if (Test-ChangeWindow -InstanceId $Instance.Id) { return 'Foreign' }

    if ($script:MmCommentReadable -eq $false)                { return 'Unknown' }

    $why = $null
    try {
        $w = $Instance.GetMaintenanceWindow()
        if ($null -eq $w) {
            $why = 'GetMaintenanceWindow() returned nothing for an object that is in maintenance mode'
        } else {
            # Property name differs across SDK versions; missing members read as
            # $null in PowerShell rather than throwing, so trying both is safe.
            $c = $w.Comments
            if ($null -eq $c) { $c = $w.Comment }

            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$c)) {
                # Every window this script sets carries $Marker, so once this SDK
                # has shown it DOES surface comments, a blank one cannot be ours.
                if ($script:MmCommentReadable -eq $true) { return 'Foreign' }

                # Until then a blank is no evidence either way - an administrator
                # leaving it empty, or an SDK that never fills it in. It must NOT
                # fall through to the latch below: that used to switch ownership
                # off for the whole run whenever the first window read was blank.
                return 'Unknown'
            } else {
                $script:MmCommentReadable = $true
                if ([string]$c -like "*$Marker*") { return 'Ours' }
                return 'Foreign'
            }
        }
    } catch {
        $why = $_.Exception.Message
    }

    if ($null -eq $script:MmCommentReadable) {
        $script:MmCommentReadable = $false
        Write-Log "Maintenance window ownership cannot be determined here ($why); every window will be treated as this script's own, exactly as before." 'WARN'
    }
    return 'Unknown'
}

# Re-reads an extend set from SCOM and advances Row.EndUtc ONLY for machines
# whose window demonstrably moved. Returns the ones still unconfirmed.
#
# The old code set $extended once and then stamped the new EndUtc on every row
# in the set, including rows Get-SCOMMaintenanceMode never returned an entry
# for. That is the same failure the per-instance path warns about: the state
# file claims a window the machine does not have, and nothing notices until the
# real one lapses.
function Confirm-Extended {
    param([object[]]$Candidates, [datetime]$NewEndUtc)

    $out = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    if (-not $Candidates -or $Candidates.Count -eq 0) { return $out.ToArray() }

    # An extend read on the same tick as the write can still show the old end
    # time, which sends the machine down the per-instance retry path and, if it
    # reads stale twice, logs a failure on an extend that worked.
    #
    # So read FIRST and pay the settle wait only for rows that have not moved.
    # This runs inside the shutdown mutex, and a flat sleep here is time the
    # APPLY loop cannot run. Rows confirmed on the first look never enter the
    # second, so Row.EndUtc and Stats.Extended are written exactly once per row.
    $tolerance = $NewEndUtc.AddMinutes(-5)
    $round     = @($Candidates)
    $fresh     = Get-InstanceMap -Ids @($round | ForEach-Object { $_.Row.InstanceId })

    for ($attempt = 0; ; $attempt++) {
        $out = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
        foreach ($t in $round) {
            $f = $fresh[[string]$t.Row.InstanceId]
            if (-not $f -or -not $f.InMaintenanceMode) { $out.Add($t); continue }

            # This is the one place holding both a window and the end time we asked
            # for, so it is where the Unspecified-kind question gets settled.
            $end = Get-MaintenanceEndUtc -Instance $f -CalibrateAgainstUtc $NewEndUtc
            if ($null -eq $end) {
                if ($script:MmWindowReadable -eq $false) {
                    # In MM is the most this SDK will prove. Trust it, as the old
                    # code did unconditionally, but only for a machine we have just
                    # confirmed really is in maintenance mode.
                    $t.Row.EndUtc = $NewEndUtc.ToString('o')
                    $script:Stats.Extended++
                } else {
                    $out.Add($t)
                }
                continue
            }

            if ($end -ge $tolerance) {
                # Record the window SCOM actually holds, not the one we asked for.
                $t.Row.EndUtc = $end.ToString('o')
                $script:Stats.Extended++
            } else {
                $out.Add($t)
            }
        }

        # Everything confirmed, the settle look is already spent, or settling is
        # switched off: this is the answer. The per-instance attempt that follows
        # a failed confirm IS the retry, and it is harmless to repeat.
        if ($out.Count -eq 0 -or $attempt -ge 1 -or $VerifySettleMs -le 0) { return $out.ToArray() }

        Write-Verbose "$($out.Count) extend(s) had not settled; re-reading after $VerifySettleMs ms."
        $round = $out.ToArray()
        Start-Sleep -Milliseconds $VerifySettleMs
        $fresh = Get-InstanceMap -Ids @($round | ForEach-Object { $_.Row.InstanceId })
    }
}

# Batched form of Resolve-WindowsComputer: one SDK round trip per 200 names
# instead of one per alert. A MaxBatch burst used to cost MaxBatch round trips,
# which was the last unbatched SDK call in the script.
function Get-WindowsComputerMap {
    param([string[]]$Fqdns)

    $map   = @{}
    $names = @($Fqdns | Where-Object { -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($_) } | Select-Object -Unique)
    if ($names.Count -eq 0) { return $map }

    $chunk = 200
    for ($i = 0; $i -lt $names.Count; $i += $chunk) {
        $slice = @($names[$i..([Math]::Min($i + $chunk, $names.Count) - 1)])

        $found = @()
        try {
            $found = @(Get-SCOMClassInstance -Name $slice -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Batched Windows Computer lookup failed for a chunk of $($slice.Count), falling back per name: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
            foreach ($n in $slice) {
                $c = Resolve-WindowsComputer -Fqdn $n
                if ($c) { $map[$n] = $c }
            }
            continue
        }

        # Hashtable keys are case-insensitive, which is what the old -ieq
        # comparison against DisplayName was doing.
        $byName = @{}
        foreach ($f in $found) {
            if (-not $f -or [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($f.DisplayName)) { continue }
            if (-not $byName.ContainsKey($f.DisplayName)) {
                $byName[$f.DisplayName] = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
            }
            $byName[$f.DisplayName].Add($f)
        }

        # The fallback rule is the one place in this script that can select an
        # object nobody intended, and until now it did so without leaving a
        # trace. It is not being changed - it is being made visible, so that
        # "which machine did it put into maintenance mode, and why that object"
        # has an answer in the log instead of a theory.
        $viaFallback = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
        $ambiguous   = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]

        foreach ($n in $slice) {
            $cands = $byName[$n]
            if (-not $cands -or $cands.Count -eq 0) { continue }
            # Same two selection rules as Resolve-WindowsComputer, in the same order.
            $c = @($cands | Where-Object { $_.FullName -like 'Microsoft.Windows.Computer:*' }) | Select-Object -First 1
            if (-not $c) {
                $loose = @($cands | Where-Object { [string]::IsNullOrEmpty($_.Path) })
                $c     = $loose | Select-Object -First 1
                if ($c) {
                    $viaFallback.Add($n)
                    # More than one unhosted candidate: the first was taken, and
                    # nothing here proves it is the Windows Computer.
                    if ($loose.Count -gt 1) { $ambiguous.Add($n) }
                }
            }
            if ($c) { $map[$n] = $c }
        }

        if ($viaFallback.Count -gt 0) {
            Write-Log ("{0} name(s) matched no 'Microsoft.Windows.Computer:*' FullName and were resolved by the unhosted-object fallback: {1}{2}" -f
                       $viaFallback.Count, ((@($viaFallback) | Select-Object -First 10) -join ', '),
                       $(if ($viaFallback.Count -gt 10) { ', ...' } else { '' })) 'WARN'
        }
        if ($ambiguous.Count -gt 0) {
            Write-Log ("{0} name(s) had MORE THAN ONE unhosted candidate; the first was taken and it may not be the Windows Computer object: {1}{2}" -f
                       $ambiguous.Count, ((@($ambiguous) | Select-Object -First 10) -join ', '),
                       $(if ($ambiguous.Count -gt 10) { ', ...' } else { '' })) 'ERROR'
        }
    }
    return $map
}

# Applies MM to a set of instances. The caller verifies against SCOM before
# trusting that it took.
function Invoke-MaintenanceMode {
    param([object[]]$Instances, [datetime]$EndUtc, [string]$Comment)
    if (-not $Instances -or $Instances.Count -eq 0) { return }

    if ($DryRun) {
        foreach ($i in $Instances) { Write-Plan 'MM-APPLY' $i.DisplayName "until $EndUtc UTC" }
        return
    }

    $startUtc = [datetime]::UtcNow
    $targets  = $Instances

    if (-not $UseSdkForApply) {
        try {
            # Cmdlet takes local time. Batched server-side.
            Start-SCOMMaintenanceMode -Instance $targets -EndTime $EndUtc.ToLocalTime() `
                -Reason PlannedOther -Comment $Comment
            return
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Batched MM cmdlet failed, falling back per-instance: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'

            # The batch may have applied to SOME of them before throwing.
            # Calling ScheduleMaintenanceMode on an object already in MM throws,
            # so re-read and skip those - otherwise a partial success turns into
            # a pile of spurious ERRORs and a non-zero exit on a pass that
            # mostly worked.
            try {
                $fresh = Get-InstanceMap -Ids @($targets | ForEach-Object { $_.Id.ToString() })
                if ($fresh.Count -gt 0) {
                    $remaining = @($targets | Where-Object {
                        $f = $fresh[$_.Id.ToString()]
                        -not ($f -and $f.InMaintenanceMode)
                    })
                    $skipped = $targets.Count - $remaining.Count
                    if ($skipped -gt 0) {
                        Write-Log "$skipped machine(s) were already in MM from the partial batch - skipping them in the fallback."
                    }
                    $targets = $remaining
                }
            } catch {
                Write-Log "Could not re-read instances before the fallback: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
            }
        }
    }

    foreach ($i in $targets) {
        try {
            # SDK takes UTC, and lets us name the traversal depth explicitly.
            $i.ScheduleMaintenanceMode(
                $startUtc, $EndUtc,
                [Microsoft.EnterpriseManagement.Monitoring.MaintenanceModeReason]::PlannedOther,
                $Comment,
                [Microsoft.EnterpriseManagement.Common.TraversalDepth]::Recursive)
        } catch {
            Write-Log "MM failed for $($i.DisplayName): $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
        }
    }
}

# ==========================================================================
# Shared checks that decide whether anything is systemically wrong
# ==========================================================================
function Test-StateLoss {
    param([int]$CurrentCount)
    $state = Get-NotifyState
    $last  = ConvertTo-Int $state['LastTrackedCount']

    # Recovered from the backup. Still worth an email - something removed or
    # corrupted the primary, and that wants explaining - but it is not the
    # emergency the message below describes, because nothing was orphaned.
    if ($script:StateRecoveredFrom) {
        $body = @(
            "The tracked-state file was missing or unreadable, and was rebuilt"
            "from the backup written by the previous pass."
            ""
            "  Path       : $StateFile"
            "  Recovered  : $CurrentCount machine(s) from $script:StateRecoveredFrom"
            ""
            "No machines were orphaned. At most one pass of changes was lost, and"
            "the next sweep re-derives that from SCOM."
            ""
            "Worth finding out what removed or corrupted the primary file - a"
            "backup agent, antivirus, a disk problem, or someone tidying up. If"
            "the original was corrupt rather than missing it has been kept at"
            "${StateFile}.corrupt."
        ) -join "`r`n"

        Send-Notification -Key 'StateRecovered' -Severity 'WARN' `
            -Subject "Tracked-state file rebuilt from backup ($CurrentCount machines)" `
            -Body $body -MinIntervalMinutes 1440
        return
    }

    # A file that reads EMPTY is a legitimate drain. A file that has GONE, or
    # will not parse, while we believed machines were suppressed, is loss:
    # everything in it is now in MM and untracked until the window expires.
    if (-not $script:StateFileExisted -and $last -ge $StateLossThreshold) {
        $body = @(
            "The tracked-state file is missing or unreadable."
            ""
            "  Path                          : $StateFile"
            "  Machines tracked at last pass : $last"
            ""
            "Those machines are still in maintenance mode but are no longer"
            "tracked, so nothing will release or extend them. They will surface"
            "as normal heartbeat alerts within $MaintenanceHours hours when their"
            "windows expire."
            ""
            "The backup at ${StateFile}.bak was tried automatically and could not"
            "be used either - it is missing, empty, or unreadable. That is why"
            "this is the serious message rather than the recovery one."
        ) -join "`r`n"

        Send-Notification -Key 'StateLoss' -Severity 'ERROR' `
            -Subject "Tracked-state file lost ($last machines were suppressed)" `
            -Body $body -MinIntervalMinutes 1440
    }
}

function Update-TrackedCount {
    param([int]$Count)
    $state = Get-NotifyState
    $state['LastTrackedCount'] = $Count
    Set-NotifyState -State $state
}

function Test-SuppressionCeiling {
    param([int]$TrackedCount, [int]$AppliedThisPass = 0)

    if ($TrackedCount -ge $TrackedCeiling) {
        $body = @(
            "$TrackedCount machines are currently suppressed, at or above the"
            "configured ceiling of $TrackedCeiling."
            ""
            "Suppression is now being REFUSED for anything above this ceiling,"
            "so machines beyond it will alert normally. A spurious source of"
            "Event 1074 alerts is the usual cause; a genuine outage larger than"
            "the ceiling is the other."
            ""
            "Worth checking:"
            "  - the '$AlertName' rule, for an unexpected volume of new alerts"
            "  - whether a genuine site-wide outage is in progress"
            "  - $StateFile for machines you do not expect to see"
        ) -join "`r`n"

        Send-Notification -Key 'RunawaySuppression' -Severity 'ERROR' `
            -Subject "$TrackedCount machines suppressed (ceiling $TrackedCeiling)" `
            -Body $body -MinIntervalMinutes 60
    }

    if ($ApplyBurstCeiling -gt 0 -and $AppliedThisPass -ge $ApplyBurstCeiling) {
        $body = @(
            "$AppliedThisPass machines entered maintenance mode in a single pass,"
            "at or above the configured burst ceiling of $ApplyBurstCeiling."
            ""
            "Legitimate on a large planned shutdown. Worth a look if not."
        ) -join "`r`n"

        Send-Notification -Key 'ApplyBurst' -Severity 'WARN' `
            -Subject "$AppliedThisPass machines suppressed in one pass" `
            -Body $body -MinIntervalMinutes 60
    }
}

function Test-MmFailureRate {
    param([int]$Failed, [int]$Attempted, [string]$Phase)
    if ($Failed -le 0) { return }

    $systemic = ($Failed -ge $MmFailureAlertCount) -or
                ($Attempted -ge 2 -and ($Failed / [double]$Attempted) -ge 0.5)
    if (-not $systemic) { return }

    $body = @(
        "Maintenance mode failed for $Failed of $Attempted machine(s) during $Phase."
        ""
        "At this rate the cause is systemic rather than per-machine - the usual"
        "suspects are the service account losing SCOM rights, the SDK service"
        "being unavailable, or the config service being overloaded."
        ""
        "While this persists, gracefully shut down machines are NOT being"
        "suppressed, which is the alert storm this script exists to prevent."
        ""
        "See $LogPath for the per-machine errors."
    ) -join "`r`n"

    Send-Notification -Key 'MmFailure' -Severity 'ERROR' `
        -Subject "Maintenance mode failing wholesale ($Failed of $Attempted, $Phase)" `
        -Body $body -MinIntervalMinutes 60
}

# ==========================================================================
# SWEEP - release, extend, reapply, record
# ==========================================================================
function Invoke-SweepPass {
    # First, so an unreadable exclusion file stops the pass before anything moves.
    $exclusions = @(Get-ExclusionList)

    # @() matters: with exactly one row tracked, Windows PowerShell 5.1 hands
    # back a bare [pscustomobject], which has no .Count there.
    $tracked = @(Get-TrackedState)
    Test-StateLoss -CurrentCount $tracked.Count

    if ($tracked.Count -eq 0) {
        Write-Log 'Nothing currently suppressed.'
        Update-TrackedCount -Count 0
        $script:Stats.Tracked = 0
        return
    }

    $nowUtc = [datetime]::UtcNow
    $probe  = Test-AgentReachability -ComputerNames ($tracked.Fqdn)

    # One batched fetch gives every tracked machine's current InMaintenanceMode,
    # so lapsed windows are caught on the next sweep rather than up to 16 hours
    # later when the state file happens to say the window is nearly up.
    $instMap = Get-InstanceMap -Ids ($tracked.InstanceId)

    # Diagnostic census of what the state file actually holds: row count,
    # distinct FQDNs, and the classes behind the tracked InstanceIds. Free -
    # $instMap is already in hand - and wrapped so it can never fail the pass.
    try {
        $distinctFqdn = @($tracked.Fqdn | Sort-Object -Unique).Count
        if ($instMap.Count -gt 0) {
            $byClass = @($instMap.Values |
                         Group-Object { ($_.FullName -split ':')[0] } -NoElement |
                         Sort-Object Count -Descending)
            Write-Log ("Tracked census: {0} row(s), {1} distinct FQDN(s), {2} resolved - classes: {3}" -f
                       $tracked.Count, $distinctFqdn, $instMap.Count,
                       (($byClass | Select-Object -First 8 |
                         ForEach-Object { '{0} x{1}' -f $_.Name, $_.Count }) -join ', '))
        } else {
            Write-Log ("Tracked census: {0} row(s), {1} distinct FQDN(s), none resolved in the management group." -f
                       $tracked.Count, $distinctFqdn) 'WARN'
        }
    } catch {
        Write-Log "Tracked census failed: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
    }

    # Ownership, worked out up front in two rounds. A blank window comment only
    # reads as foreign once some window has proved comments are readable here,
    # so deciding row by row would judge a blank met early in the list on less
    # evidence than one met late. Round two re-asks only the Unknowns.
    $ownerOf = @{}
    foreach ($row in $tracked) {
        $i = $instMap[[string]$row.InstanceId]
        if ($i -and $i.InMaintenanceMode) { $ownerOf[[string]$row.InstanceId] = Get-MaintenanceOwner -Instance $i }
    }
    if ($script:MmCommentReadable -eq $true) {
        foreach ($k in @($ownerOf.Keys | Where-Object { $ownerOf[$_] -eq 'Unknown' })) {
            $ownerOf[$k] = Get-MaintenanceOwner -Instance $instMap[$k]
        }
    }

    $keep      = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    $toRelease = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    $toExtend  = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    $toReapply = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    $toReport  = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    $heldForeign    = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]   # down, someone else's window
    $droppedForeign = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]   # back up, someone else's window
    $droppedExcluded = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]  # excluded, nothing of ours to undo
    $pingOnly  = 0
    $agentUp   = 0

    foreach ($row in $tracked) {
        $inst = $instMap[[string]$row.InstanceId]
        if (-not $inst) {
            Write-Log "$($row.Fqdn) no longer exists in the management group - dropped from tracking." 'WARN'
            Add-EventRecord -Type 'InstanceVanished' -Fqdn $row.Fqdn -Detail $row.AppliedUtc
            continue
        }

        $pingStreak  = ConvertTo-Int $row.PingStreak
        $pingOnlyRun = ConvertTo-Int $row.PingOnlyStreak
        $isUp        = $false
        $viaPingOnly = $false

        $p       = $probe[$row.Fqdn]
        $pingOk  = ($p -and $p.Ping)
        $agentOk = ($p -and $p.Agent)

        # DNS is the decommissioning signal, and it was being computed and
        # thrown away. A switched-off machine keeps its DNS record; a
        # decommissioned one loses it. Stamped here, reported in the digest.
        if ($p -and $p.Resolved) { $row.LastDnsOkUtc = $nowUtc.ToString('o') }

        if ($agentOk) {
            # Authoritative: the machine is up and HealthService is listening.
            $isUp = $true; $pingStreak = 0; $pingOnlyRun = 0
            $agentUp++
        }
        elseif ($pingOk) {
            $pingStreak++
            if ($SkipAgentPortCheck) {
                $isUp = ($pingStreak -ge $PingStreakForRelease)
            }
            else {
                $pingOnlyRun++
                $pingOnly++
                if ($pingOnlyRun -ge $ReleaseAfterPingOnlySweeps) {
                    # Long past a plausible boot window. Release it: a live
                    # machine whose agent is not listening is a real fault and
                    # belongs in someone's queue, not hidden in maintenance mode.
                    $isUp = $true; $viaPingOnly = $true
                    Write-Log ("{0}: answers ping but nothing on TCP {1} after {2} sweeps - releasing so the agent fault surfaces." -f
                               $row.Fqdn, $AgentPort, $pingOnlyRun) 'WARN'
                } else {
                    Write-Verbose ("{0}: pings but not TCP {1} (sweep {2} of {3}) - probably still booting, staying suppressed." -f
                                   $row.Fqdn, $AgentPort, $pingOnlyRun, $ReleaseAfterPingOnlySweeps)
                }
            }
        }
        else {
            $pingStreak = 0; $pingOnlyRun = 0
        }

        $row.PingStreak     = $pingStreak
        $row.PingOnlyStreak = $pingOnlyRun

        # Whose window is this? Only meaningful while the object is actually in
        # one; if it is not, the reapply path below is about to make it ours.
        $owner   = if ($inst.InMaintenanceMode) { $ownerOf[[string]$row.InstanceId] } else { 'Ours' }
        $foreign = ($owner -eq 'Foreign')

        if ($foreign) {
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$row.ForeignSinceUtc)) {
                $row.ForeignSinceUtc = $nowUtc.ToString('o')
                Write-Log ("{0} is in a maintenance window this script did not set - leaving it alone." -f $row.Fqdn) 'WARN'
                Add-EventRecord -Type 'ForeignMm' -Fqdn $row.Fqdn -Detail 'detected'
            }
        } else {
            # Back under our management (or never left it).
            $row.ForeignSinceUtc = ''
        }

        # On the exclusion list - added after this machine was suppressed. It
        # must not sit in maintenance mode, so take it out of OUR window whether
        # it is up or not (released first, ahead of the MaxReleaseBatch cap).
        # Someone else's window is still theirs. Either way, stop tracking it:
        # never extended, never reapplied.
        $hit = Get-ExclusionMatch -Fqdn $row.Fqdn -Exclusions $exclusions
        if ($hit) {
            if ($inst.InMaintenanceMode -and -not $foreign) {
                $toRelease.Insert(0, [pscustomobject]@{ Row = $row; Instance = $inst; ViaPingOnly = $false; Excluded = $hit })
            } else {
                $droppedExcluded.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Row = $row; Excluded = $hit
                                                        Why = $(if ($foreign) { "in a window this script did not set - left in place" } else { "not in maintenance mode" }) })
            }
            continue
        }

        if ($isUp) {
            if ($foreign) {
                # The machine is back AND someone else owns the window. Calling
                # StopMaintenanceMode here would pull it out of a window an
                # administrator set deliberately. There is nothing left for this
                # script to do for it, so it stops tracking it instead.
                $droppedForeign.Add($row)
                Add-EventRecord -Type 'ForeignRelease' -Fqdn $row.Fqdn -Detail 'back up, left in place'
                continue
            }
            $toRelease.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Row = $row; Instance = $inst; ViaPingOnly = $viaPingOnly; Excluded = $null })
            continue
        }

        # Still down. Report it if it has been dormant a long time.
        $appliedUtc = ConvertTo-Utc $row.AppliedUtc
        if ($appliedUtc -and ($nowUtc - $appliedUtc).TotalDays -ge $ReviewAfterDays) {
            $lastReported = ConvertTo-Utc $row.LastReportedUtc
            if (-not $lastReported -or ($nowUtc - $lastReported).TotalDays -ge $ReportEveryDays) {
                $lastDns = ConvertTo-Utc $row.LastDnsOkUtc
                $toReport.Add([pscustomobject]@{
                    ReportedUtc = $nowUtc.ToString('o')
                    Fqdn        = $row.Fqdn
                    DownSince   = $row.AppliedUtc
                    DaysDown    = [math]::Round(($nowUtc - $appliedUtc).TotalDays, 1)
                    DaysNoDns   = $(if ($lastDns) { [math]::Round(($nowUtc - $lastDns).TotalDays, 1) } else { 'never resolved' })
                })
                $row.LastReportedUtc = $nowUtc.ToString('o')
            }
        }

        if (-not $inst.InMaintenanceMode) {
            # MM lapsed, or was ended by hand, while the machine is still down.
            # If a foreign window is what lapsed, management resumes here - which
            # is the whole reason a hands-off machine stays tracked.
            $toReapply.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Row = $row; Instance = $inst })
        } elseif ($foreign) {
            # Still down, still in someone else's window. No extend: pushing the
            # end time out would overwrite whatever they set it to.
            $heldForeign.Add($row)
        } else {
            $endUtc = ConvertTo-Utc $row.EndUtc
            if (-not $endUtc -or ($endUtc - $nowUtc).TotalHours -le $ExtendWhenWithinHours) {
                $toExtend.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Row = $row; Instance = $inst })
            }
        }

        $keep.Add($row)
    }

    # --- Dry run stops here ------------------------------------------------
    # Everything above is read-only: the reachability probe, the instance
    # fetch, the ownership check. Everything below changes SCOM or the state
    # files. One gate, rather than a condition at each of the eight mutation
    # points, so there is no path that can half-apply.
    if ($DryRun) {
        $newEnd = $nowUtc.AddHours($MaintenanceHours)
        foreach ($t in $toRelease) {
            if ($t.Excluded) {
                Write-Plan 'RELEASE' $t.Row.Fqdn "on the exclusion list ('$($t.Excluded)') - out of this script's window, then untracked"
                continue
            }
            $ap = ConvertTo-Utc $t.Row.AppliedUtc
            $d  = if ($ap) { [math]::Round(($nowUtc - $ap).TotalDays, 2) } else { 0 }
            Write-Plan 'RELEASE' $t.Row.Fqdn ("agent answered; down $d days" + $(if ($t.ViaPingOnly) { ' (ping-only fallback)' } else { '' }))
        }
        foreach ($x in $droppedExcluded) { Write-Plan 'UNTRACK' $x.Row.Fqdn "on the exclusion list ('$($x.Excluded)'), $($x.Why)" }
        foreach ($t in $toExtend)  { Write-Plan 'EXTEND'  $t.Row.Fqdn "window to $($newEnd.ToString('u'))" }
        foreach ($t in $toReapply) { Write-Plan 'REAPPLY' $t.Row.Fqdn "maintenance mode has lapsed while still down" }
        foreach ($r in $heldForeign)    { Write-Plan 'HANDS-OFF' $r.Fqdn "still down, in a window this script did not set" }
        foreach ($r in $droppedForeign) { Write-Plan 'UNTRACK'   $r.Fqdn "back up, but in a window this script did not set - left in place" }
        foreach ($m in $toReport)  { Write-Plan 'REVIEW'  $m.Fqdn "dormant $($m.DaysDown) days" }

        $noChange = $keep.Count - $toExtend.Count - $toReapply.Count - $heldForeign.Count
        Write-Log ("DRYRUN SWEEP: {0} tracked - {1} release, {2} extend, {3} reapply, {4} hands off, {5} untrack, {6} no change. Nothing was changed." -f
                   $tracked.Count, $toRelease.Count, $toExtend.Count, $toReapply.Count,
                   $heldForeign.Count, ($droppedForeign.Count + $droppedExcluded.Count), [Math]::Max(0, $noChange))
        $script:Stats.Tracked = $tracked.Count
        return
    }

    # --- Excluded, nothing to undo -----------------------------------------
    foreach ($x in $droppedExcluded) {
        Write-Log ("{0} is on the exclusion list ('{1}') - dropped from tracking; {2}." -f $x.Row.Fqdn, $x.Excluded, $x.Why) 'WARN'
        Add-EventRecord -Type 'ExcludedUntracked' -Fqdn $x.Row.Fqdn -Detail $x.Excluded
    }

    # --- Release -----------------------------------------------------------
    # There is no Stop-SCOMMaintenanceMode cmdlet, and no bulk stop. Per object.
    # Capped, because Monday morning is the mass event: hundreds returning at
    # once, each stop triggering config recalculation.
    if ($toRelease.Count -gt 0) {
        $deferred = @()
        if ($toRelease.Count -gt $MaxReleaseBatch) {
            Write-Log "$($toRelease.Count) machine(s) ready to release; taking $MaxReleaseBatch this sweep." 'WARN'
            $deferred  = @($toRelease[$MaxReleaseBatch..($toRelease.Count - 1)])
            $toRelease = [System.Collections.Generic.List[object]]@($toRelease[0..($MaxReleaseBatch - 1)])
        }
        foreach ($d in $deferred) { $keep.Add($d.Row) }   # retry next sweep

        $stopUtc    = [datetime]::UtcNow
        $stopCalled = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
        foreach ($t in $toRelease) {
            if (-not $t.Instance.InMaintenanceMode) {
                # Already out by this instance's reckoning - but the handle is
                # from the top of the sweep, so it still goes through the
                # verification below rather than being dropped on trust.
                $stopCalled.Add($t)
                continue
            }
            try {
                $t.Instance.StopMaintenanceMode(
                    $stopUtc,
                    [Microsoft.EnterpriseManagement.Common.TraversalDepth]::Recursive)
                $stopCalled.Add($t)
            } catch {
                Write-Log "Release failed for $($t.Row.Fqdn): $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
                $keep.Add($t.Row)          # keep tracking it, retry next sweep
            }
        }

        # Verify, the way apply and reapply already do. Release was the one
        # operation taking "did not throw" as proof. A stop that silently does
        # not take drops the machine from tracking while it is still in
        # maintenance mode - blind for the rest of the window, with nothing
        # left watching it and no retry.
        $released   = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
        $stopFailed = 0
        if ($stopCalled.Count -gt 0) {
            $check = Get-VerifiedInstanceMap -ExpectInMaintenanceMode $false `
                         -Ids @($stopCalled | ForEach-Object { $_.Row.InstanceId })
            foreach ($t in $stopCalled) {
                $fresh = $check[[string]$t.Row.InstanceId]
                if ($fresh -and $fresh.InMaintenanceMode) {
                    Write-Log "Release did not take for $($t.Row.Fqdn) - still in maintenance mode; kept tracked for the next sweep." 'ERROR'
                    Add-EventRecord -Type 'MmFailed' -Fqdn $t.Row.Fqdn -Detail 'release'
                    $keep.Add($t.Row)
                    $stopFailed++
                    continue
                }

                # Out of MM, or gone from the management group entirely. Either
                # way it is no longer suppressed and no longer ours to track.
                $released.Add($t.Row.Fqdn)
                if ($t.Excluded) {
                    # Not a return from outage - it may well still be down - so
                    # kept out of the Released records the digest averages.
                    Write-Log ("{0} is on the exclusion list ('{1}') - taken out of maintenance mode and dropped from tracking." -f $t.Row.Fqdn, $t.Excluded) 'WARN'
                    Add-EventRecord -Type 'ExcludedRelease' -Fqdn $t.Row.Fqdn -Detail $t.Excluded
                    continue
                }
                $appliedUtc = ConvertTo-Utc $t.Row.AppliedUtc
                $days = if ($appliedUtc) { [math]::Round(($stopUtc - $appliedUtc).TotalDays, 2) } else { 0 }
                Add-EventRecord -Type $(if ($t.ViaPingOnly) { 'PingOnlyRelease' } else { 'Released' }) `
                                -Fqdn $t.Row.Fqdn -Detail $days
            }
            $script:Stats.MmFailures += $stopFailed
            Test-MmFailureRate -Failed $stopFailed -Attempted $stopCalled.Count -Phase 'release'
        }

        if ($released.Count -gt 0) {
            $script:Stats.Released += $released.Count
            Write-Log "Released MM for $($released.Count): $(($released | Select-Object -First 20) -join ', ')"
        }
    }

    # --- Extend ------------------------------------------------------------
    if ($toExtend.Count -gt 0) {
        $newEnd  = $nowUtc.AddHours($MaintenanceHours)
        $pending = $toExtend.ToArray()

        # --- Attempt 1: the batched cmdlet ---------------------------------
        # Chunked, because the extend set can be most of the tracked population
        # and this was the last unbounded SDK call left in the sweep.
        try {
            # Set-SCOMMaintenanceMode takes MaintenanceWindow entries, NOT instances.
            $instances = @($pending | ForEach-Object { $_.Instance })
            $chunk     = 200
            for ($i = 0; $i -lt $instances.Count; $i += $chunk) {
                $slice   = @($instances[$i..([Math]::Min($i + $chunk, $instances.Count) - 1)])
                $entries = @(Get-SCOMMaintenanceMode -Instance $slice -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)
                if ($entries.Count -eq 0) {
                    Write-Log "No active MM entries returned for a chunk of $($slice.Count) - those fall through to the per-instance path." 'WARN'
                    continue
                }
                Set-SCOMMaintenanceMode -MaintenanceModeEntry $entries -EndTime $newEnd.ToLocalTime() `
                    -Comment "$Marker - still down, window extended"
            }
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Batch extend failed, falling back per-instance: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        }

        # Only rows whose window demonstrably moved get their EndUtc advanced.
        $pending   = @(Confirm-Extended -Candidates $pending -NewEndUtc $newEnd)
        $confirmed = $toExtend.Count - $pending.Count
        if ($confirmed -gt 0) {
            Write-Log "Extended MM for $confirmed machine(s) to $newEnd UTC."
        }

        # --- Attempt 2: per-instance, only for what the batch did not take --
        if ($pending.Count -gt 0) {
            Write-Log "Batch extend confirmed $confirmed of $($toExtend.Count); retrying $($pending.Count) per-instance." 'WARN'
            foreach ($t in $pending) {
                try {
                    # SDK method, UTC.
                    $t.Instance.UpdateMaintenanceMode(
                        $newEnd,
                        [Microsoft.EnterpriseManagement.Monitoring.MaintenanceModeReason]::PlannedOther,
                        "$Marker - still down, window extended",
                        [Microsoft.EnterpriseManagement.Common.TraversalDepth]::Recursive)
                } catch {
                    Write-Log "Extend failed for $($t.Row.Fqdn): $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
                }
            }

            # Still only the confirmed ones get a new EndUtc. Advancing it on a
            # failed extend hides the machine for another window while its real
            # MM quietly expires - which is exactly what the batch path used to
            # do to every machine in the set.
            $stillPending = @(Confirm-Extended -Candidates $pending -NewEndUtc $newEnd)
            foreach ($t in $stillPending) {
                Write-Log "Extend did not take for $($t.Row.Fqdn) - EndUtc left as-is so the next sweep retries." 'ERROR'
                Add-EventRecord -Type 'MmFailed' -Fqdn $t.Row.Fqdn -Detail 'extend'
            }
            if ($stillPending.Count -gt 0) {
                $script:Stats.MmFailures += $stillPending.Count
                Test-MmFailureRate -Failed $stillPending.Count -Attempted $toExtend.Count -Phase 'extend'
            }
        }
    }

    # --- Reapply lapsed ----------------------------------------------------
    if ($toReapply.Count -gt 0) {
        $newEnd = $nowUtc.AddHours($MaintenanceHours)
        Write-Log "Re-applying lapsed MM for $($toReapply.Count) machine(s)." 'WARN'
        Invoke-MaintenanceMode -Instances @($toReapply.Instance) -EndUtc $newEnd `
            -Comment "$Marker - reapplied, still down"

        $check  = Get-VerifiedInstanceMap -ExpectInMaintenanceMode $true -Ids @($toReapply.Row.InstanceId)
        $failed = 0
        foreach ($t in $toReapply) {
            $fresh = $check[[string]$t.Row.InstanceId]
            if ($fresh -and $fresh.InMaintenanceMode) {
                $t.Row.EndUtc = $newEnd.ToString('o')
                $script:Stats.Reapplied++
                Add-EventRecord -Type 'Reapplied' -Fqdn $t.Row.Fqdn
            } else {
                Write-Log "Reapply did not take for $($t.Row.Fqdn) - will retry next sweep." 'ERROR'
                Add-EventRecord -Type 'MmFailed' -Fqdn $t.Row.Fqdn -Detail 'reapply'
                $t.Row.EndUtc = ''      # force another attempt next sweep
                $failed++
            }
        }
        $script:Stats.MmFailures += $failed
        Test-MmFailureRate -Failed $failed -Attempted $toReapply.Count -Phase 'reapply'
    }

    # --- Foreign windows ---------------------------------------------------
    if ($heldForeign.Count -gt 0 -or $droppedForeign.Count -gt 0) {
        Write-Log ("{0} machine(s) left alone in maintenance windows this script did not set; {1} dropped from tracking after coming back." -f
                   $heldForeign.Count, $droppedForeign.Count) 'WARN'
    }

    # --- Port-probe sanity -------------------------------------------------
    $script:Stats.PingOnly += $pingOnly
    if ($pingOnly -gt 0) {
        Write-Log ("{0} machine(s) answered ping with nothing listening on TCP {1} this sweep." -f
                   $pingOnly, $AgentPort) 'WARN'

        # Denominator is machines that ANSWERED this sweep, not everything
        # tracked. Machines that are genuinely down do not answer ping at all,
        # so measuring against the whole tracked population made this guard
        # essentially unfireable: 20 ping-only out of 300 tracked is 7%, nowhere
        # near the ratio, even with the port firewalled shut estate-wide.
        $answered = $pingOnly + $agentUp
        if ($answered -ge 10 -and ($pingOnly / [double]$answered) -ge $PortBlindRatio) {
            $pct = [math]::Round(100 * $pingOnly / [double]$answered)
            $body = @(
                "$pingOnly of the $answered tracked machines that answered ping this"
                "sweep ($pct%) had nothing listening on TCP $AgentPort."
                ""
                "At that proportion the likely cause is the port being blocked"
                "between this management server and the agents - a firewall or"
                "GPO change - rather than that many agents genuinely being down."
                ""
                "Effect while this persists: machines that have come back stay"
                "suppressed for $ReleaseAfterPingOnlySweeps sweeps before being"
                "released on the fallback rule, so live machines are briefly"
                "invisible to monitoring."
                ""
                "Workaround if the port cannot be reopened quickly:"
                "  add -SkipAgentPortCheck to the SWEEP task (release then falls"
                "  back to $PingStreakForRelease consecutive pings)."
            ) -join "`r`n"

            Send-Notification -Key 'PortBlind' -Severity 'WARN' `
                -Subject "TCP $AgentPort unreachable for $pct% of suppressed machines" `
                -Body $body -MinIntervalMinutes 1440
        }
    }

    # --- Dormant report ----------------------------------------------------
    if ($toReport.Count -gt 0) {
        try {
            New-ParentDirectory -Path $ReportPath
            if (Test-Path $ReportPath) {
                # The row shape gained ReportedUtc and DaysNoDns. Export-Csv
                # -Append refuses a header mismatch, so retire an old-schema
                # file once rather than failing this report on every sweep.
                $head = @(Get-Content -Path $ReportPath -TotalCount 1)
                if ($head.Count -gt 0 -and $head[0] -notmatch 'ReportedUtc') {
                    Move-Item -Path $ReportPath -Destination "$ReportPath.old" -Force
                    Write-Log "Dormant report was on the old schema - moved to ${ReportPath}.old and started a new one." 'WARN'
                }
            }
            $toReport | Export-Csv -Path $ReportPath -NoTypeInformation -Append
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Could not append to the dormant report: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        }
        foreach ($m in $toReport) {
            Write-Log "REVIEW: $($m.Fqdn) suppressed for $($m.DaysDown) days - decommissioned?" 'WARN'
        }
        # Emitted to stdout so the scheduled task output can be mailed on.
        $toReport | Format-Table -AutoSize | Out-String | Write-Output
    }

    Set-TrackedState -Rows $keep
    Update-TrackedCount -Count $keep.Count
    $script:Stats.Tracked = $keep.Count
    Test-SuppressionCeiling -TrackedCount $keep.Count
}

# ==========================================================================
# APPLY - pick up new shutdown alerts
# ==========================================================================
function Invoke-ApplyPass {
    # First, so an unreadable exclusion file stops the pass before anything moves.
    $exclusions = @(Get-ExclusionList)

    $sinceUtc = [datetime]::UtcNow.AddMinutes(-$LookbackMinutes)
    # Round-trip format. The short 'yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ' form is the usual
    # cause of a criteria parse failure here.
    $stamp    = $sinceUtc.ToString('yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss.fffffffZ')
    $safeName = $AlertName -replace "'", "''"

    # Server-side criteria. Never replace this with Get-SCOMAlert | Where-Object;
    # on this management group that drags back every open alert in the estate.
    $criteria = "Name = '$safeName' AND ResolutionState = 0 AND TimeRaised >= '$stamp'"

    try {
        # Sorted, because Get-SCOMAlert makes no ordering promise and MaxBatch
        # slicing an unordered set can starve the same alerts every pass until
        # they age out of the lookback and are lost for good.
        $alerts = @(Get-SCOMAlert -Criteria $criteria | Sort-Object TimeRaised)
    } catch {
        Write-Log "Alert query failed (check the TimeRaised format): $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
        return
    }

    $tracked = [System.Collections.ArrayList]@(Get-TrackedState)
    Test-StateLoss -CurrentCount $tracked.Count
    $script:Stats.Tracked = $tracked.Count

    if ($alerts.Count -eq 0) {
        Write-Log 'No new shutdown alerts.'
        Update-TrackedCount -Count $tracked.Count
        Test-SuppressionCeiling -TrackedCount $tracked.Count
        return
    }
    if ($alerts.Count -gt $MaxBatch) {
        Write-Log "$($alerts.Count) shutdown alerts pending; taking the $MaxBatch oldest this pass." 'WARN'
        $alerts = $alerts[0..($MaxBatch - 1)]
    }

    $known = @{}
    foreach ($t in $tracked) { $known[$t.Fqdn] = $true }

    $candidates = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]   # need MM
    $alreadyMM  = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]   # track only
    $closeOnly  = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]   # dupes

    $notifyState   = Get-NotifyState
    $orphanTouched = $false

    # Name every alert first, so the Windows Computer lookup can be batched.
    # It used to be one SDK round trip per alert, which at MaxBatch was 100
    # sequential round trips on the pass that could least afford them.
    $pairs = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    foreach ($alert in $alerts) {
        # PrincipalName carries the agent FQDN. NetbiosDomainName is the NetBIOS
        # domain, so composing with it yields SERVER01.CONTOSO, not the FQDN.
        $fqdn = $alert.PrincipalName
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($fqdn)) {
            if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($alert.NetbiosComputerName)) {
                Write-Log "Alert $($alert.Id) carries no computer name - skipped." 'WARN'
                continue
            }
            $fqdn = if ($alert.NetbiosDomainName) {
                        '{0}.{1}' -f $alert.NetbiosComputerName, $alert.NetbiosDomainName
                    } else { $alert.NetbiosComputerName }
            Write-Log "Alert $($alert.Id) had no PrincipalName; composed '$fqdn' from NetBIOS names." 'WARN'
        }
        $pairs.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Fqdn = $fqdn; Alert = $alert })
    }

    # Excluded machines are not touched at all: no maintenance mode, not
    # tracked, and the alert is left OPEN so the shutdown shows in the console.
    # It is re-seen every pass for the lookback, so it is logged once per
    # invocation and recorded in the event history at most once a day.
    $excluded = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    if ($exclusions.Count -gt 0) {
        $rest = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
        foreach ($p in $pairs) {
            $hit = Get-ExclusionMatch -Fqdn $p.Fqdn -Exclusions $exclusions
            if ($hit) { $excluded.Add([pscustomobject]@{ Fqdn = $p.Fqdn; Alert = $p.Alert; Entry = $hit }) }
            else      { $rest.Add($p) }
        }
        $pairs = $rest
    }
    foreach ($x in $excluded) {
        if ($script:ExcludedLogged.ContainsKey($x.Fqdn)) { continue }
        $script:ExcludedLogged[$x.Fqdn] = $true
        $script:Stats.Excluded++
        Write-Log ("{0} shut down but is on the exclusion list ('{1}') - not put into maintenance mode; alert {2} left open." -f
                   $x.Fqdn, $x.Entry, $x.Alert.Id) 'WARN'
        $lastSeen = ConvertTo-Utc ([string]$notifyState['Excluded'][$x.Fqdn])
        if (-not $lastSeen -or ([datetime]::UtcNow - $lastSeen).TotalHours -ge 24) {
            Add-EventRecord -Type 'Excluded' -Fqdn $x.Fqdn -Detail $x.Entry
            $notifyState['Excluded'][$x.Fqdn] = [datetime]::UtcNow.ToString('o')
            $orphanTouched = $true      # same once-a-day map handling as orphans
        }
    }

    # Only alerts for machines we are not already tracking need an instance.
    $computerMap = Get-WindowsComputerMap -Fqdns @($pairs |
                       Where-Object { -not $known[$_.Fqdn] } |
                       ForEach-Object { $_.Fqdn })

    foreach ($p in $pairs) {
        $fqdn  = $p.Fqdn
        $alert = $p.Alert

        if ($known[$fqdn]) { $closeOnly.Add($alert); continue }   # already suppressed

        $computer = $computerMap[$fqdn]
        if (-not $computer) {
            Write-Log "No Windows Computer instance found for '$fqdn' - skipped." 'WARN'
            $script:Stats.Orphans++

            # Record at most once a day per machine. The alert stays at
            # resolution state 0 and is re-seen every pass for the whole
            # lookback, so recording unconditionally would write hundreds of
            # identical rows a day into the event history.
            $lastSeen = ConvertTo-Utc ([string]$notifyState['Orphans'][$fqdn])
            if (-not $lastSeen -or ([datetime]::UtcNow - $lastSeen).TotalHours -ge 24) {
                Add-EventRecord -Type 'OrphanAlert' -Fqdn $fqdn -Detail "alert $($alert.Id)"
                $notifyState['Orphans'][$fqdn] = [datetime]::UtcNow.ToString('o')
                $orphanTouched = $true
            }
            continue
        }

        # Owner and EndUtc are filled in below: 'Ours' with the window we set
        # for anything we put into MM ourselves, and whatever is actually there
        # for anything that was already in it.
        $entry = [pscustomobject]@{ Fqdn = $fqdn; Alert = $alert; Instance = $computer
                                    Owner = 'Ours'; EndUtc = '' }
        if ($computer.InMaintenanceMode) { $alreadyMM.Add($entry) } else { $candidates.Add($entry) }
        $known[$fqdn] = $true
    }

    if ($orphanTouched) {
        # Prune anything not seen for a month so this cannot grow forever.
        $cut = [datetime]::UtcNow.AddDays(-30)
        foreach ($map in @($notifyState['Orphans'], $notifyState['Excluded'])) {
            foreach ($k in @($map.Keys)) {
                $t = ConvertTo-Utc ([string]$map[$k])
                if (-not $t -or $t -lt $cut) { $map.Remove($k) }
            }
        }
        Set-NotifyState -State $notifyState
    }

    # A machine already in maintenance mode when its alert arrives is tracked,
    # but that window is not one we set and its end is not the end we would have
    # chosen. The old code stamped $endUtc on it anyway and cleared
    # ForeignSinceUtc, so the state file asserted a window that might expire
    # hours earlier than claimed, and the next sweep would happily EXTEND an
    # administrator's window as though it were ours. Ask once, here, and record
    # what is actually there - the dry run reads the same answers rather than
    # working ownership out separately.
    foreach ($e in $alreadyMM) {
        $e.Owner = Get-MaintenanceOwner -Instance $e.Instance
        $realEnd = Get-MaintenanceEndUtc -Instance $e.Instance
        # Empty, not invented. The sweep reads a missing EndUtc as "due an
        # extend", which is right for a window of ours and never consulted for
        # a foreign one.
        $e.EndUtc = $(if ($realEnd) { $realEnd.ToString('o') } else { '' })
    }
    # A blank comment only reads as foreign once some window has proved
    # comments are readable; give any blank met before that proof a second look.
    if ($script:MmCommentReadable -eq $true) {
        foreach ($e in @($alreadyMM | Where-Object { $_.Owner -eq 'Unknown' })) {
            $e.Owner = Get-MaintenanceOwner -Instance $e.Instance
        }
    }

    $processed = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    foreach ($a in $closeOnly) { $processed.Add($a) }

    $endUtc  = [datetime]::UtcNow.AddHours($MaintenanceHours)
    $applied = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    foreach ($e in $alreadyMM) { $applied.Add($e) }

    if ($DryRun) {
        foreach ($x in $excluded) {
            Write-Plan 'EXCLUDED' $x.Fqdn "on the exclusion list ('$($x.Entry)') - no maintenance mode, alert $($x.Alert.Id) left open"
        }
        foreach ($e in $candidates) {
            Write-Plan 'ENTER-MM' $e.Fqdn "until $($endUtc.ToString('u')), then close alert $($e.Alert.Id)"
        }
        foreach ($e in $alreadyMM) {
            $note = $(if ($e.Owner -eq 'Foreign') { "already in a window this script did not set" } else { "already in maintenance mode" })
            $when = $(if ($e.EndUtc) { "window ends $((ConvertTo-Utc $e.EndUtc).ToString('u'))" } else { "window end not readable" })
            Write-Plan 'TRACK-ONLY' $e.Fqdn "$note, $when; would track and close alert $($e.Alert.Id)"
        }
        Write-Log ("DRYRUN APPLY: {0} alert(s) in scope - {1} would enter maintenance mode, {2} already in it, {3} duplicate(s) closed, {4} orphan(s), {5} excluded. Nothing was changed." -f
                   $alerts.Count, $candidates.Count, $alreadyMM.Count, $closeOnly.Count, $script:Stats.Orphans, $excluded.Count)
        $script:Stats.Tracked = $tracked.Count
        return
    }

    # ---- the ceiling is a fence, not a sign -------------------------------
    # This used to warn and then carry on suppressing, which is the wrong way
    # round for a script whose worst failure is blinding the monitoring while
    # every log line still reads as success. Machines refused here keep their
    # alert at resolution state 0, so they alert normally AND are retried next
    # pass once the tracked population drops back below the ceiling.
    if ($candidates.Count -gt 0) {
        $headroom = $TrackedCeiling - $tracked.Count
        if ($headroom -lt 0) { $headroom = 0 }

        if ($candidates.Count -gt $headroom) {
            $refused = $candidates.Count - $headroom
            $kept    = if ($headroom -gt 0) { @(@($candidates)[0..($headroom - 1)]) } else { @() }
            $candidates = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
            foreach ($k in $kept) { $candidates.Add($k) }

            Write-Log ("Tracked ceiling reached: {0} suppressed against a ceiling of {1}. Refusing maintenance mode for {2} machine(s) this pass; their alerts are left open." -f
                       $tracked.Count, $TrackedCeiling, $refused) 'ERROR'

            Send-Notification -Key 'SuppressionRefused' -Severity 'ERROR' `
                -Subject "Suppression refused for $refused machine(s) - ceiling $TrackedCeiling reached" `
                -Body (@(
                    "$($tracked.Count) machines are suppressed, at or above the ceiling of $TrackedCeiling."
                    ""
                    "$refused machine(s) that would otherwise have entered maintenance mode this pass"
                    "were refused. Their alerts remain open and will be seen again next pass."
                    ""
                    "The ceiling is deliberately absolute: suppressing past it would blind the"
                    "monitoring for up to $MaintenanceHours hours with nothing in the log to show it."
                    ""
                    "Either a spurious source of Event 1074 alerts is running, or a genuine outage"
                    "larger than the ceiling is in progress. Check $StateFile and the '$AlertName' rule."
                ) -join "`r`n") -MinIntervalMinutes 60
        }
    }

    $mmFailed = 0
    if ($candidates.Count -gt 0) {
        Invoke-MaintenanceMode -Instances @($candidates.Instance) -EndUtc $endUtc `
            -Comment "$Marker - orderly shutdown detected (Event 1074)"

        # Verify before tracking or closing. Recording a machine as suppressed
        # when MM did not take, and closing its alert, buries it for the whole
        # window - it looks handled and alerts the entire time.
        # ForEach-Object, not $candidates.Instance.Id.ToString(). PowerShell
        # enumerates PROPERTY access over a collection but not METHOD calls, so
        # that form ran ToString() on the Object[] itself and passed the literal
        # string "System.Object[]" - no GUID parsed, empty map, and every
        # machine on the pass was reported as an MM failure on a pass where MM
        # had actually applied cleanly.
        $check = Get-VerifiedInstanceMap -ExpectInMaintenanceMode $true `
                     -Ids @($candidates | ForEach-Object { $_.Instance.Id.ToString() })
        foreach ($e in $candidates) {
            $fresh = $check[$e.Instance.Id.ToString()]
            if ($fresh -and $fresh.InMaintenanceMode) {
                $e.Owner  = 'Ours'                    # we just set this one
                $e.EndUtc = $endUtc.ToString('o')
                $applied.Add($e)
            } else {
                # Leave the alert open at ResolutionState 0 so the next pass retries.
                Write-Log "MM did not take for $($e.Fqdn) - alert left open for retry." 'ERROR'
                Add-EventRecord -Type 'MmFailed' -Fqdn $e.Fqdn -Detail 'apply'
                $mmFailed++
            }
        }
        $script:Stats.MmFailures += $mmFailed
        Test-MmFailureRate -Failed $mmFailed -Attempted $candidates.Count -Phase 'apply'
    }

    $nowStamp = [datetime]::UtcNow.ToString('o')
    foreach ($e in $applied) {
        $tracked.Add([pscustomobject]@{
            Fqdn            = $e.Fqdn
            InstanceId      = $e.Instance.Id.ToString()
            AppliedUtc      = $nowStamp
            EndUtc          = $e.EndUtc          # the window that IS there, not one we assumed
            PingStreak      = 0
            PingOnlyStreak  = 0
            LastReportedUtc = ''
            LastDnsOkUtc    = ''      # unknown until the first sweep probes it
            ForeignSinceUtc = $(if ($e.Owner -eq 'Foreign') { $nowStamp } else { '' })
        }) | Out-Null
        $processed.Add($e.Alert)

        if ($e.Owner -eq 'Foreign') {
            # Tracked so the reapply path picks it up if that window lapses while
            # the machine is still down, but never extended and never released -
            # the same contract the sweep already honours.
            Write-Log ("{0} was already in a maintenance window this script did not set - tracked, but left alone." -f $e.Fqdn) 'WARN'
            Add-EventRecord -Type 'ForeignMm' -Fqdn $e.Fqdn -Detail 'already in MM when the alert arrived'
        }
        Add-EventRecord -Type 'Applied' -Fqdn $e.Fqdn
    }

    if ($applied.Count -gt 0) {
        Set-TrackedState -Rows $tracked
        $script:Stats.Applied += $applied.Count
        Write-Log "Maintenance mode confirmed for $($applied.Count) machine(s) until $endUtc UTC."
    }

    if ($processed.Count -gt 0) {
        # Closing the alert is verified the same way a maintenance-mode write is,
        # and for the same reason: "the SDK call did not throw" is not proof.
        #
        # The failure used to be logged 'WARN'. Write-Log only counts 'ERROR'
        # toward $script:Errs, so a pass on which EVERY closure failed still
        # ended on the clean Information event, the Missing Event monitor stayed
        # satisfied, and the watchdog reported a healthy run while handled
        # shutdown alerts accumulated unclosed in the console. That is the one
        # failure shape this script is built to avoid, so it is an ERROR now.
        #
        # It is not fatal to the machine: a failed closure leaves the alert at
        # ResolutionState 0, the next pass re-queries it, finds the FQDN already
        # tracked and closes it on the closeOnly path. The ERROR exists to make
        # a PERSISTENT failure visible rather than silent.
        $alertIds  = [guid[]]@($processed | ForEach-Object { $_.Id })
        $setFailed = $false
        try {
            Set-SCOMAlert -Alert $processed.ToArray() -ResolutionState $ProcessedResolutionState `
                -Comment "$Marker - processed"
        } catch {
            $setFailed = $true
            Write-Log "Could not update resolution state for $($processed.Count) alert(s): $($_.Exception.Message)" 'ERROR'
        }

        if (-not $setFailed) {
            try {
                $unclosed = @(Get-SCOMAlert -Id $alertIds -ErrorAction Stop |
                              Where-Object { $_.ResolutionState -ne $ProcessedResolutionState })

                # Same settle problem as Get-VerifiedInstanceMap: a read on the
                # same tick as the write can still show the old state, and
                # calling that a failure puts an ERROR on a pass where nothing
                # was wrong. Pay the wait only for the stragglers - the normal
                # case costs one read and no sleep. This is inside the mutex.
                if ($unclosed.Count -gt 0 -and $VerifySettleMs -gt 0) {
                    Write-Verbose "$($unclosed.Count) alert(s) had not settled; re-reading after $VerifySettleMs ms."
                    Start-Sleep -Milliseconds $VerifySettleMs
                    $unclosed = @(Get-SCOMAlert -Id ([guid[]]@($unclosed | ForEach-Object { $_.Id })) -ErrorAction Stop |
                                  Where-Object { $_.ResolutionState -ne $ProcessedResolutionState })
                }

                if ($unclosed.Count -gt 0) {
                    Write-Log ("{0} of {1} alert(s) did not take resolution state {2}. They stay open; the next pass sees them again and closes them on the duplicate path." -f
                               $unclosed.Count, $alertIds.Count, $ProcessedResolutionState) 'ERROR'
                    foreach ($u in ($unclosed | Select-Object -First 10)) {
                        Add-EventRecord -Type 'AlertCloseFailed' -Fqdn ([string]$u.PrincipalName) -Detail ([string]$u.Id)
                    }
                } else {
                    Write-Verbose "Resolution state $ProcessedResolutionState confirmed for $($alertIds.Count) alert(s)."
                }
            } catch {
                # Could not READ them back. Says nothing about whether the write
                # took, so it is not an error on its own.
                Write-Log "Could not verify alert resolution states: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
            }
        }
    }

    $script:Stats.Tracked = $tracked.Count
    Update-TrackedCount -Count $tracked.Count
    Test-SuppressionCeiling -TrackedCount $tracked.Count -AppliedThisPass $applied.Count
}

# ==========================================================================
# DIGEST - weekly report, and event-history pruning
# ==========================================================================
function Invoke-DigestPass {
    $nowUtc  = [datetime]::UtcNow
    $tracked = @(Get-TrackedState)      # @(): a lone row has no .Count on 5.1
    $events  = Get-EventRecords -Days 7

    $applied         = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -eq 'Applied' })
    $released        = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -eq 'Released' -or $_.Type -eq 'PingOnlyRelease' })
    $pingOnlyRel     = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -eq 'PingOnlyRelease' })
    $vanished        = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -eq 'InstanceVanished' })
    $orphans         = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -eq 'OrphanAlert' })
    $mmFailed        = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -eq 'MmFailed' })
    $reapplied       = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -eq 'Reapplied' })
    $excludedEv      = @($events | Where-Object { $_.Type -like 'Excluded*' })

    # Outage durations, from the Released records.
    $durations = @()
    foreach ($r in $released) {
        $d = 0.0
        if ([double]::TryParse([string]$r.Detail, [ref]$d) -and $d -gt 0) { $durations += $d }
    }
    $avgDays  = if ($durations.Count -gt 0) { [math]::Round(($durations | Measure-Object -Average).Average, 2) } else { 0 }
    $maxDays  = if ($durations.Count -gt 0) { [math]::Round(($durations | Measure-Object -Maximum).Maximum, 2) } else { 0 }
    $medDays  = 0
    if ($durations.Count -gt 0) {
        $sorted  = @($durations | Sort-Object)
        # Even counts take the mean of the two middle values; the old code took
        # the upper-middle one, which is not a median.
        $medDays = if ($sorted.Count % 2 -eq 0) {
            [math]::Round((($sorted[($sorted.Count / 2) - 1] + $sorted[$sorted.Count / 2]) / 2), 2)
        } else {
            [math]::Round($sorted[[int][math]::Floor($sorted.Count / 2)], 2)
        }
    }

    # Long returns - machines that came back after being dormant a long time.
    $longReturns = @($released | Where-Object {
        $d = 0.0
        [double]::TryParse([string]$_.Detail, [ref]$d) -and $d -ge $ReviewAfterDays
    } | Sort-Object { [double]$_.Detail } -Descending)

    # Currently dormant.
    $dormant = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[object]
    foreach ($row in $tracked) {
        $a = ConvertTo-Utc $row.AppliedUtc
        if ($a -and ($nowUtc - $a).TotalDays -ge $ReviewAfterDays) {
            $lastDns   = ConvertTo-Utc $row.LastDnsOkUtc
            $daysNoDns = $(if ($lastDns) { [math]::Round(($nowUtc - $lastDns).TotalDays, 1) } else { $null })
            $dormant.Add([pscustomobject]@{
                Fqdn      = $row.Fqdn
                DownSince = $(if ($a) { $a.ToString('yyyy-MM-dd') } else { '' })
                DaysDown  = [math]::Round(($nowUtc - $a).TotalDays, 1)
                DaysNoDns = $(if ($null -eq $daysNoDns) { 'never resolved' } else { $daysNoDns })
                DnsGone   = (($null -eq $daysNoDns) -or ($daysNoDns -ge $ReviewAfterDays))
            })
        }
    }
    $dormantSorted = @($dormant | Sort-Object DaysDown -Descending)

    # Attach the dormant list as a CSV.
    $attachment = @()
    if ($dormantSorted.Count -gt 0) {
        try {
            $csv = Join-Path $env:TEMP ("ScomDormantAgents_{0}.csv" -f $nowUtc.ToString('yyyyMMdd'))
            $dormantSorted | Export-Csv -Path $csv -NoTypeInformation -Force
            $attachment = @($csv)
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Could not build the dormant CSV attachment: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        }
    }

    $lines = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]
    $lines.Add("SCOM shutdown suppression - week ending $($nowUtc.ToString('yyyy-MM-dd')) UTC")
    $lines.Add('')
    $lines.Add('SUPPRESSION')
    $lines.Add(("  Currently suppressed      : {0}" -f $tracked.Count))
    $lines.Add(("  Entered maintenance mode  : {0}" -f $applied.Count))
    $lines.Add(("  Released                  : {0}" -f $released.Count))
    $lines.Add(("  Lapsed windows reapplied  : {0}" -f $reapplied.Count))
    $lines.Add('')
    $lines.Add('OUTAGE LENGTH (released this week, days)')
    $lines.Add(("  Average {0}   Median {1}   Longest {2}" -f $avgDays, $medDays, $maxDays))
    $lines.Add('')

    if ($dormantSorted.Count -gt 0) {
        $lines.Add("DORMANT $ReviewAfterDays+ DAYS - candidates for decommissioning ($($dormantSorted.Count))")
        foreach ($d in ($dormantSorted | Select-Object -First 40)) {
            $lines.Add(("  {0,-45} {1,6} days   since {2}{3}" -f $d.Fqdn, $d.DaysDown, $d.DownSince,
                        $(if ($d.DnsGone) { '   [no DNS record]' } else { '' })))
        }
        if ($dormantSorted.Count -gt 40) {
            $lines.Add("  ... and $($dormantSorted.Count - 40) more; full list attached.")
        }
        $lines.Add('')

        $dnsGone = @($dormantSorted | Where-Object { $_.DnsGone })
        if ($dnsGone.Count -gt 0) {
            $lines.Add("OF THOSE, NO DNS RECORD FOR $ReviewAfterDays+ DAYS ($($dnsGone.Count))")
            $lines.Add("  Dormant AND unresolvable. A machine that is merely switched off")
            $lines.Add("  usually keeps its DNS record; a decommissioned one usually loses it.")
            $lines.Add("  Treat that as the strongest evidence in this report, not as proof:")
            $lines.Add("  scavenging, a DNS change and a record pulled ahead of a rebuild all")
            $lines.Add("  look identical from here. Confirm before removing a SCOM object.")
            foreach ($d in ($dnsGone | Select-Object -First 40)) {
                $lines.Add(("  {0,-45} {1,6} days down" -f $d.Fqdn, $d.DaysDown))
            }
            $lines.Add('')
        }
    }

    $foreignRows = @($tracked | Where-Object { -not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace([string]$_.ForeignSinceUtc) })
    if ($foreignRows.Count -gt 0) {
        $lines.Add("IN A MAINTENANCE WINDOW THIS SCRIPT DID NOT SET ($($foreignRows.Count))")
        $lines.Add("  Someone - an administrator, or another automation - owns the window")
        $lines.Add("  on these. They are not being extended or released, so nothing here")
        $lines.Add("  fights whoever set them. Management resumes automatically if that")
        $lines.Add("  window lapses while the machine is still down.")
        foreach ($r in ($foreignRows | Select-Object -First 20)) {
            $since = ConvertTo-Utc $r.ForeignSinceUtc
            $lines.Add(("  {0,-45} since {1}" -f $r.Fqdn, $(if ($since) { $since.ToString('yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm') } else { 'unknown' })))
        }
        if ($foreignRows.Count -gt 20) { $lines.Add("  ... and $($foreignRows.Count - 20) more.") }
        $lines.Add('')
    }

    if ($longReturns.Count -gt 0) {
        $lines.Add("RETURNED AFTER $ReviewAfterDays+ DAYS ($($longReturns.Count))")
        foreach ($r in ($longReturns | Select-Object -First 20)) {
            $lines.Add(("  {0,-45} was down {1} days" -f $r.Fqdn, $r.Detail))
        }
        $lines.Add('')
    }

    if ($pingOnlyRel.Count -gt 0) {
        $lines.Add("RELEASED WITH THE AGENT STILL SILENT ($($pingOnlyRel.Count))")
        $lines.Add("  These answered ping but never opened TCP $AgentPort. Each is a")
        $lines.Add("  live machine whose agent is not running - worth investigating.")
        foreach ($r in ($pingOnlyRel | Select-Object -First 20)) { $lines.Add("  $($r.Fqdn)") }
        $lines.Add('')
    }

    if ($vanished.Count -gt 0) {
        $lines.Add("DELETED FROM SCOM WHILE SUPPRESSED ($($vanished.Count))")
        $lines.Add("  Normal if they were decommissioned properly. Worth a look if not.")
        foreach ($v in ($vanished | Select-Object -First 20)) { $lines.Add("  $($v.Fqdn)") }
        $lines.Add('')
    }

    if ($orphans.Count -gt 0) {
        $uniqueOrphans = @($orphans | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Fqdn -Unique)
        $lines.Add("SHUTDOWN ALERTS WITH NO WINDOWS COMPUTER OBJECT ($($uniqueOrphans.Count) machines)")
        $lines.Add("  These raised a shutdown alert but could not be resolved to an")
        $lines.Add("  object, so they were NOT suppressed. Usually an agent removed")
        $lines.Add("  from SCOM but still reporting, or a name-format mismatch.")
        foreach ($o in ($uniqueOrphans | Select-Object -First 20)) { $lines.Add("  $o") }
        $lines.Add('')
    }

    if ($excludedEv.Count -gt 0) {
        $uniqueExcluded = @($excludedEv | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Fqdn -Unique)
        $lines.Add("EXCLUDED MACHINES ($($uniqueExcluded.Count))")
        $lines.Add("  On the exclusion list, so never put into maintenance mode. Either")
        $lines.Add("  they shut down (alert left open), or they were added to the list")
        $lines.Add("  while suppressed and have been taken out of it.")
        foreach ($o in ($uniqueExcluded | Select-Object -First 20)) { $lines.Add("  $o") }
        $lines.Add('')
    }

    if ($mmFailed.Count -gt 0) {
        $lines.Add("MAINTENANCE MODE FAILURES ($($mmFailed.Count) occurrences)")
        $lines.Add("  See $LogPath for detail.")
        $lines.Add('')
    }

    $body = $lines -join "`r`n"
    Write-Output $body

    $to = if ($MailReportTo.Count -gt 0) { $MailReportTo } else { $MailTo }
    Send-Notification -Key 'WeeklyDigest' -Severity 'INFO' `
        -Subject ("Weekly suppression digest - {0} suppressed, {1} dormant {2}+ days" -f
                  $tracked.Count, $dormantSorted.Count, $ReviewAfterDays) `
        -Body $body -MinIntervalMinutes 360 -To $to -Attachment $attachment

    foreach ($a in $attachment) { try { Remove-Item $a -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch { } }

    # --- Prune the event history ------------------------------------------
    if ($DryRun) { Write-Log 'DRYRUN: event history and dormant report not pruned.' }
    if ((Test-Path $EventCsvPath) -and -not $DryRun) {
        try {
            $cutoff = $nowUtc.AddDays(-$EventRetentionDays)
            $all    = @(Import-Csv -Path $EventCsvPath -ErrorAction Stop)
            $kept   = @($all | Where-Object {
                $t = ConvertTo-Utc $_.Utc
                $t -and $t -ge $cutoff
            })
            if ($kept.Count -lt $all.Count) {
                $tmp = "$EventCsvPath.tmp"
                if ($kept.Count -gt 0) {
                    $kept | Export-Csv -Path $tmp -NoTypeInformation -Force
                } else {
                    Set-Content -Path $tmp -Value 'Utc,Type,Fqdn,Detail' -Force
                }
                Move-Item -Path $tmp -Destination $EventCsvPath -Force
                Write-Log "Pruned $($all.Count - $kept.Count) event record(s) older than $EventRetentionDays days."
            }
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Event history prune failed: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        }
    }

    # --- Prune the dormant report -----------------------------------------
    # The sweep appends to this on every report cycle and nothing ever trimmed
    # it, so it grew without bound and accumulated a duplicate row per machine
    # per ReportEveryDays. Rows written before ReportedUtc existed carry no
    # timestamp and go on the first prune; the digest above says the same thing.
    if ((Test-Path $ReportPath) -and -not $DryRun) {
        try {
            $cutoff = $nowUtc.AddDays(-$EventRetentionDays)
            $all    = @(Import-Csv -Path $ReportPath -ErrorAction Stop)
            $kept   = @($all | Where-Object {
                $t = ConvertTo-Utc $_.ReportedUtc
                $t -and $t -ge $cutoff
            })
            if ($kept.Count -lt $all.Count) {
                $tmp = "$ReportPath.tmp"
                if ($kept.Count -gt 0) {
                    $kept | Export-Csv -Path $tmp -NoTypeInformation -Force
                } else {
                    Set-Content -Path $tmp -Value 'ReportedUtc,Fqdn,DownSince,DaysDown,DaysNoDns' -Force
                }
                Move-Item -Path $tmp -Destination $ReportPath -Force
                Write-Log "Pruned $($all.Count - $kept.Count) dormant-report row(s) older than $EventRetentionDays days."
            }
        } catch {
            Write-Log "Dormant report prune failed: $($_.Exception.Message)" 'WARN'
        }
    }

    $script:Stats.Tracked = $tracked.Count
}

